window.PondGateBookDataEn = {"version":"1.3.4","lang":"en","title":"PondGate Detention-Pond Routing in Practice — User Manual and Worked Examples","chapters":[{"id":"bk-c1","part":"","title":"Preface","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c1\">Preface</h2><p>This book is the handbook of PondGate. It has two parts.</p><p>Part I is the user manual. It follows the order of the software screens and explains what to enter, what to press and what to read in each module. First-time users can start with Chapter 4, \"Five-minute start\", and look up the other chapters when needed.</p><p>Part II is a set of worked examples. It starts from the hydraulic principles of detention ponds. It then uses the public benchmark cases built into the software to work step by step through routing, interpretation, design screening and cross-checks with external models. Each chapter has four parts: principle, step-by-step example, check and exercises. Answers to the exercises are in Appendix C.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c1-s1\">Scope of this book</h3><p>PondGate computes the water level, storage and outflow of one detention pond under a known inflow hydrograph. It does not compute rainfall–runoff, channel routing, long-term continuous simulation or ponds in series.</p><p>Every result in this book holds only under the declared inputs and assumptions. Neither the software nor this book replaces design codes, site investigation or the judgement of a professional engineer. Every tick and record marked \"teaching assumption\" is for practice only and must not be used in real projects.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c1-s2\">Where the numbers come from</h3><p>Every result number in Part II was read from this version of the software after a real run. An independent small program then recomputed it for checking. If you get a different number with the same software version, first check that your inputs are exactly the same as in the book.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c1-s3\">Version and citation</h3><p>This English edition matches PondGate v1.3.4. The screenshots were taken in the English interface. The source code is public on GitHub (hongyaoming-cloud/detention-pond-dss), and the Zenodo DOIs of each version are listed in Appendix D.</p><p>Author: Yao-Ming Hong, Professor, Department of Natural Resources and Environmental Studies, National Dong Hwa University, Hualien, Taiwan.</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c1-s1","title":"Scope of this book"},{"id":"bk-c1-s2","title":"Where the numbers come from"},{"id":"bk-c1-s3","title":"Version and citation"}]},{"id":"bk-c2","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c2\">Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do</h2><p>PondGate is detention-pond routing software that runs in a web browser. For one detention pond under a known inflow hydrograph, it computes how the water level, storage and outflow change over time. It keeps the inputs, method and results of every run as a traceable record.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c2-s1\">1.1 The method in one sentence</h3><p>The software solves the level-pool (storage continuity) equation:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">dS/dt = Qin(t) − Qout(H)</div><p>Here S is the storage in the pond, H is the water level, Qin is the inflow and Qout is the outlet discharge. The equation is solved with the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method at a fixed step (RK4, Δt = 2 s). Chapters 11 to 13 explain the principle and show hand checks.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c2-s2\">1.2 What it can do</h3><ul><li>Inflow–outflow routing of a single detention pond, giving the peak reduction, the maximum water level and the event-added detention volume.</li><li>\"Constrained design screening\" and \"continuous optimization\" under a declared allowable outflow and allowable level, to find feasible pond areas and outlet sizes.</li><li>Download of a traceable report with the inputs, results and scope of application.</li><li>Numerical comparison with HEC-HMS and SWMM results.</li><li>After signing in, saving a case as a project and sharing it with colleagues in the same company (Chapter 2).</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c2-s3\">1.3 What it cannot do</h3><ul><li>Rainfall–runoff calculation: the user must supply the inflow hydrograph.</li><li>Channel routing, long-term continuous simulation or ponds in series.</li><li>Field calibration: agreement with other models is called \"numerical consistency\" only.</li><li>Engineering approval: results are not a final design. Engineering responsibility stays with qualified professionals.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c2-s4\">1.4 The software will not say \"pass\" for you</h3><p>When no feasible design exists within the current limits and search bounds, PondGate shows \"Blocked\". It lists the dominant limits and the directions for revision, and it does not show \"pass\". This is deliberate: it stops users from getting a result that looks acceptable when the evidence is not enough. Chapter 18 uses an example to show how to read a blocked result.</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c2-s1","title":"1.1 The method in one sentence"},{"id":"bk-c2-s2","title":"1.2 What it can do"},{"id":"bk-c2-s3","title":"1.3 What it cannot do"},{"id":"bk-c2-s4","title":"1.4 The software will not say \"pass\" for you"}]},{"id":"bk-c3","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c3\">Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects</h2><p>You must sign in to use the official PondGate website. Your company administrator creates your account. After signing in, you have all calculation functions, and you can also save a case as a project, continue it later and share it with colleagues in the same company. A teaching or demonstration version without sign-in has the same calculation functions but no project storage.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c3-s1\">2.1 Four roles</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Role</th><th>Can do</th><th>Cannot do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>System administrator</td><td>Add companies and accounts for each company</td><td>Open company projects</td></tr><tr><td>Company administrator</td><td>Manage accounts of their own company and view all company projects</td><td>Manage other companies</td></tr><tr><td>Engineer</td><td>Create and save their own projects and share them with colleagues</td><td>Change colleagues' projects</td></tr><tr><td>Viewer</td><td>Open projects that colleagues have shared</td><td>Save</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The system administrator cannot open company projects on purpose. This keeps the engineering data of each company inside that company.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c3-s2\">2.2 First sign-in</h3><ol><li>Press \"Sign in\" at the top right of the screen.</li><li>Enter the user name and initial password that your administrator gave you.</li><li>The system asks you to change to your own password at once. The new password needs at least 8 characters and must be entered twice.</li><li>If you forget your password, ask your company administrator to reset it. The system does not send passwords.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c3-s3\">2.3 Order for creating accounts</h3><ol><li>The system administrator first adds the company.</li><li>The system administrator creates one \"company administrator\" account for that company.</li><li>The company administrator signs in and creates the engineer and viewer accounts.</li></ol><p>When creating an account, you can press \"Random password\" and then give the password to your colleague in private.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c3-s4\">2.4 Save and open projects</h3><ul><li>Engineers and company administrators can press \"Save / open projects\" to save all current inputs as a new project, with a name and a note.</li><li>If you tick \"Share with my organisation (read-only for others)\", colleagues can open the project but can only view it, not change it.</li><li>The current project name is shown at the top of the screen. When \"● unsaved changes\" appears, remember to press \"Save\".</li><li>After you open a project, results do not appear automatically. Go to Module 4 and press \"Run routing and evaluation\" to compute again. This makes sure that the results always come from the current software version.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c3-s5\">2.5 Recycle bin and activity log</h3><ul><li>A deleted project only moves to the recycle bin; it is not really removed. Press \"Restore\" in the recycle bin to get it back.</li><li>A company administrator can see the latest 300 actions under \"Accounts\", for example who created, shared or deleted which project and when.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c3-s6\">2.6 Where the data are stored</h3><p>All calculations run inside your browser, and no data are sent during a calculation. Only when you press \"Save\" are the input values of the project stored in the PondGate cloud database (Cloudflare). Passwords are kept only as encrypted hashes, and nobody (not even the system administrator) can see your password.</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c3-s1","title":"2.1 Four roles"},{"id":"bk-c3-s2","title":"2.2 First sign-in"},{"id":"bk-c3-s3","title":"2.3 Order for creating accounts"},{"id":"bk-c3-s4","title":"2.4 Save and open projects"},{"id":"bk-c3-s5","title":"2.5 Recycle bin and activity log"},{"id":"bk-c3-s6","title":"2.6 Where the data are stored"}]},{"id":"bk-c4","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 3  Screen tour","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c4\">Chapter 3  Screen tour</h2><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/guide_en/g02_01_layout.png\" alt=\"Screen layout of PondGate\"><figcaption>Screen layout of PondGate</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 3-1 shows the four main areas of PondGate.</p><ol><li>Tab bar: \"Start here\", \"Setup and routing\", \"Constrained screening\", \"Results, verification and records\" and \"Teaching and internal tests\".</li><li>Language button: when it shows \"中文\", the interface is in English.</li><li>\"? Help / AI assistant\" button, available at any time.</li><li>Work area: the content of the current page.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c4-s1\">3.1 The \"Start here\" help centre</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/guide_en/g02_02_start.png\" alt=\"The \"Start here\" page\"><figcaption>The \"Start here\" page</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 3-2 shows the \"Start here\" page. The tabs at the top are, in order: Start here, Guided lessons, User manual, Full step-by-step guide, Book (this book), Tutorial videos, Glossary and AI assistant.</p><ul><li>The \"Next step\" box changes as you work and tells you what to do now.</li><li>\"Guided lessons\" has 7 lessons from easy to harder. The system ticks each step when you finish it.</li><li>The \"?\" next to a field name explains the term.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c4-s2\">3.2 Tips</h3><ul><li>Each time you press a button, a message at the bottom right explains what just happened.</li><li>Switching the language does not change the input values. If the inputs changed after the last run, switching the language reruns the calculation with the current inputs, and the screen warns you first.</li><li>The software also works on a phone. The tab bar then moves to the top of the screen.</li></ul>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c4-s1","title":"3.1 The \"Start here\" help centre"},{"id":"bk-c4-s2","title":"3.2 Tips"}]},{"id":"bk-c5","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 4  Five-minute start","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c5\">Chapter 4  Five-minute start</h2><p>This chapter uses the \"triangular event\" demonstration case, which is loaded when the software opens, to complete your first routing run. The demonstration case is for practice only and does not stand for any real project.</p><ol><li>Open PondGate and switch to English if needed. Check that the tab bar is on \"Setup and routing\".</li><li>Scroll down to Module 4 at the bottom of the page and press \"Run routing and evaluation\".</li><li>The page switches to \"Results, verification and records\".</li><li>In the \"Beginner check\" box above the results, read what the system says about Hmax and Qp,o.</li><li>In the results table, first check that the \"Mass-balance residual ε/Vin\" is close to 0 %. Then read the peak inflow, the peak outflow and the peak water level.</li><li>Press \"Download reproducible decision-support report\" to save the record of this run to your computer.</li></ol><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/guide_en/g03_06_qchart.png\" alt=\"Inflow and routed outflow hydrographs\"><figcaption>Inflow and routed outflow hydrographs</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 4-1 shows the inflow and outflow hydrographs of the demonstration case. The outflow peak is lower than the inflow peak and comes later. This is the \"peak reduction\" and \"delay\" effect of a detention pond.</p><p>Table 4-1 lists the numbers you should see. If the numbers are the same, you did it right.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value you should see</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peak inflow Qp,i</td><td>0.1200 m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Peak outflow Qp,o</td><td>0.0903 m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Peak water level Hmax</td><td>0.413 m</td></tr><tr><td>Mass-balance residual ε/Vin</td><td>0.000 %</td></tr><tr><td>Beginner check</td><td>Not good enough</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The default allowable outflow Qallow of the demonstration case is 0.080 m³/s. The peak outflow of 0.0903 m³/s is above this value, so the \"Beginner check\" says \"Not good enough\". This is the expected result, not an operating error. Chapters 10 and 14 explain the reason and how to improve the design.</p><p>There are two ways to reset all inputs to the demonstration case. First, open \"Start here → Book\" and press \"Back to the base case\". Second, reload the web page. Both clear any unsaved changes. After a reload, switch to English again if the page opens in Chinese.</p><p>Readers who want to follow every step with a screenshot can open \"Start here → Full step-by-step guide\", Chapter 3.</p>","sec":[]},{"id":"bk-c6","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c6\">Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c6-s1\">5.1 Module 1: inflow boundary</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/en/f03_inflow.png\" alt=\"Module 1 inflow boundary\"><figcaption>Module 1 inflow boundary</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 5-1 shows Module 1. The inflow can be described in three ways.</p><ul><li>Triangular event: enter the peak inflow Qp, the time to peak Tp and the recession time Tr.</li><li>Long-duration event: enter the rise, plateau and recession times to form a trapezoidal hydrograph.</li><li>Imported time–discharge pairs: one \"time (min), discharge (m³/s)\" pair per line. The first time must be 0, times must increase, discharges must not be negative, and at least two lines are needed.</li></ul><p>The total event duration T is the sum of the parts and cannot be entered directly. Remember that the peak discharge alone is not enough: the duration and shape of the hydrograph also change the detention volume needed (Chapter 16 shows an example).</p><h3 id=\"bk-c6-s2\">5.2 Module 2: stage–storage relation</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/en/f04_storage.png\" alt=\"Module 2 storage–water-level state\"><figcaption>Module 2 storage–water-level state</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 5-2 shows Module 2. Storage can be described in two ways.</p><ul><li>Constant plan area: S(H) = A × (H − Hfloor). Only the area A is needed.</li><li>Tabulated stage–storage relation: import a two-column .csv or .txt file, with the water level (m) in the first column and the storage (m³) in the second. Routing does not extrapolate beyond the table.</li></ul><p>The floor elevation Hfloor, the initial level H0 and all outlet elevations must use the same datum. With a relative datum, set Hfloor = 0.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c6-s3\">5.3 Module 3: outlet hydraulics and free-discharge evidence</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/en/f05_outlet.png\" alt=\"Module 3 outlet hydraulics and free-discharge evidence\"><figcaption>Module 3 outlet hydraulics and free-discharge evidence</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 5-3 shows Module 3. Only two outlet types can enter formal constrained screening.</p><ul><li>Sharp-crested weir: Qout = Cw × b × (H − P)^1.5. Enter the weir coefficient Cw, the weir width b and the crest elevation P.</li><li>Rectangular opening: enter the discharge coefficient Cd, the opening area Ao, the opening height and the invert elevation. Before the water rises above the top of the opening, the opening acts like a narrow weir. After that, the discharge increases with head according to the orifice equation. Chapter 12 gives the full equations.</li></ul><p>Risers, circular pipes, stage-dependent coefficient tables and combined outlets (weir and opening together) are for \"research inspection\". You can route them and read the results, but constrained design screening is locked.</p><p>You can use the default coefficients (Cw = 1.70, Cd = 0.62) or enter calibrated site values. The defaults are suitable only for preliminary screening.</p><p>The free-discharge evidence consists of one main tick, four checks (tailwater, backwater and pipe friction, submergence, operational control) and two text records. Tick them only when you really have drawings, site checks or calculations to support them. The software does not verify these conditions for you.</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c6-s1","title":"5.1 Module 1: inflow boundary"},{"id":"bk-c6-s2","title":"5.2 Module 2: stage–storage relation"},{"id":"bk-c6-s3","title":"5.3 Module 3: outlet hydraulics and free-discharge evidence"}]},{"id":"bk-c7","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c7\">Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c7-s1\">6.1 Run</h3><p>Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" in Module 4 to start the calculation. If the button is grey, a field in Modules 1 to 3 has an unreasonable value. The fields to fix are listed above the button. Click one to jump to it; it is outlined in red.</p><p>Common causes are: Qp less than or equal to 0, an area of 0, a weir width or coefficient of 0, an outlet elevation below the floor, or no active outlet.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c7-s2\">6.2 Beginner check</h3><p>The \"Beginner check\" box at the top of the results page compares three results directly with the limits you declared: the peak water level Hmax with the allowable level Hallow, the peak outflow Qp,o with the allowable outflow Qallow, and the residual after the drain-down time Td with the allowable residual percentage.</p><ul><li>Green: all three are below their limits.</li><li>Yellow: a value equals its limit and there is no safety margin. Treat it with caution.</li><li>Red: a limit is exceeded. The box suggests a direction: a larger pond area usually lowers both the peak water level and the peak outflow; a smaller outlet lowers the peak outflow but raises the peak water level.</li></ul><p>The beginner check only compares readings. It is not an engineering approval.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c7-s3\">6.3 Order for reading the results table</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/guide_en/g03_07_table.png\" alt=\"Results table\"><figcaption>Results table</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 6-1 shows the results table. Read it in this order.</p><ol><li>Mass-balance residual ε/Vin: it should be close to 0 %. Above 0.5 %, the system asks you to solve the numerical problem first, and the results must not be used.</li><li>Peak outflow Qp,o and peak ratio RQ = Qp,o ÷ Qp,i: a smaller RQ means more peak reduction.</li><li>Peak water level Hmax and event-added detention volume ΔSmax: compare them with the allowable level and the freeboard.</li><li>Near-peak flatness FPI95: a large value means a very flat peak. A single peak time is then not reliable; use the volume-based timing measures instead.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c7-s4\">6.4 When the inputs change, the results expire</h3><p>If an input changes after a run, the screen marks the results as out of date. Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again before you interpret them. Ticking the free-discharge evidence or filling in the record fields does not make the routing results expire, because these fields do not change the computed numbers.</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c7-s1","title":"6.1 Run"},{"id":"bk-c7-s2","title":"6.2 Beginner check"},{"id":"bk-c7-s3","title":"6.3 Order for reading the results table"},{"id":"bk-c7-s4","title":"6.4 When the inputs change, the results expire"}]},{"id":"bk-c8","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c8\">Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization</h2><p>Constrained design screening answers this question: within the limits and search range I give, is there a pond area and outlet size that meets all limits at the same time?</p><h3 id=\"bk-c8-s1\">7.1 When screening is available</h3><p>Constrained screening is available only when all of the following hold.</p><ul><li>Only one sharp-crested weir or one rectangular opening is active.</li><li>The coefficients are fixed values.</li><li>The main free-discharge tick, the four checks and the two records are all complete.</li></ul><p>If a condition is missing, Module 5 lists what is missing, where to check it and what to enter. These conditions are usually confirmed by the responsible design engineer from drawings or site data.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c8-s2\">7.2 Limits to enter</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/en/f08_screening.png\" alt=\"Module 5 constrained-screening settings\"><figcaption>Module 5 constrained-screening settings</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 7-1 shows the settings of Module 5.</p><ul><li>Allowable peak outflow Qallow (m³/s) and allowable water level Hallow (m): these should come from regulations or design criteria and be written in the \"Screening-limit basis\".</li><li>Maximum drain-down time Td and permitted residual percentage: at the event end time Te plus Td, the event-added storage must not exceed this percentage of ΔSmax.</li><li>Reference level Href: used only to compute the dimensionless parameters ΠQ and ΠS. It is not a safety limit.</li><li>Search bounds: the minimum and maximum of the weir width b or the opening area Ao. For each ticked size, five equally spaced values are tested within the bounds.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c8-s3\">7.3 Run and results</h3><p>Press \"Run constrained design screening\". The page then switches to the results page. When a feasible case is found, the result lists the selected pond area, the outlet size and a comparison with each limit.</p><p>Continuous optimization searches the area A, between its minimum and maximum, together with one outlet size, and then refines locally. Its result is not a proven global optimum.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c8-s4\">7.4 No feasible case</h3><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"manual/en/f10_blocked.png\" alt=\"Blocked diagnosis when no feasible case exists\"><figcaption>Blocked diagnosis when no feasible case exists</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 7-2 shows the screen when no feasible case exists. The system shows \"Blocked\" and the dominant limits (peak outflow, maximum water level or drain-down residual), with \"Go to fix\" buttons.</p><p>Change the pond area or outlet size on an engineering basis, or widen the search bounds where there is a basis for it. Do not relax Qallow or Hallow just to get a solution.</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c8-s1","title":"7.1 When screening is available"},{"id":"bk-c8-s2","title":"7.2 Limits to enter"},{"id":"bk-c8-s3","title":"7.3 Run and results"},{"id":"bk-c8-s4","title":"7.4 No feasible case"}]},{"id":"bk-c9","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 8  Reports and traceable records","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c9\">Chapter 8  Reports and traceable records</h2><ul><li>The \"Run record for this session (read-only)\" on the results page lists the inputs, method, results and limits of this run.</li><li>\"Download reproducible decision-support report\" saves a text file with the inputs, results and scope of application. You can attach it to the design record.</li><li>When the inputs change, the old results expire. Run again before downloading.</li><li>On the \"Start here → Book\" tab you can download this book as a PDF or a single web page, and the input data of the Part II examples.</li></ul>","sec":[]},{"id":"bk-c10","part":"Part I  User Manual","title":"Chapter 9  Error messages, common questions and the AI assistant","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c10\">Chapter 9  Error messages, common questions and the AI assistant</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c10-s1\">9.1 Common questions</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Cause</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The run button is grey</td><td>A field in Modules 1 to 3 is unreasonable</td><td>Read the red list above the button and click a field name to fix it</td></tr><tr><td>Constrained screening stays locked</td><td>More than one outlet, coefficients not fixed, or incomplete free-discharge evidence</td><td>Read the list of missing conditions in Module 5</td></tr><tr><td>\"No feasible case\" appears</td><td>No design within the bounds meets all limits</td><td>Change the area or outlet according to the dominant limit; see Chapter 18</td></tr><tr><td>Mass-balance residual above 0.5 %</td><td>A numerical problem, often with extreme inputs</td><td>Check the input units and the datum; see Chapter 13</td></tr><tr><td>Results are marked out of date</td><td>Inputs changed after the run</td><td>Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"bk-c10-s2\">9.2 AI assistant</h3><p>Press \"? Help / AI assistant\" at the bottom left to open the AI assistant. It is an offline help assistant. It runs inside your browser, does not connect to any outside AI service and sends no data.</p><p>The AI assistant can do three things.</p><ol><li>Answer operating questions, for example \"What should I do next?\" or \"Why can't I press the button?\".</li><li>Explain terms, for example \"What is Qallow?\".</li><li>Search this book: type a keyword (for example \"orifice\", \"RK4\" or \"no feasible\"), and it lists the most relevant sections. Click one to jump to it.</li></ol><p>The AI assistant does not change your inputs and makes no engineering judgements. It can tell you how the numbers in the results table compare with the limits, but it will never say \"this design can be adopted\".</p>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c10-s1","title":"9.1 Common questions"},{"id":"bk-c10-s2","title":"9.2 AI assistant"}]},{"id":"bk-p12","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Part II  Worked Examples","intro":true,"html":"<h2 id=\"bk-p12\">Part II  Worked Examples</h2><p>Each chapter of Part II follows the order \"principle → step-by-step example → check → exercises\". The inputs of every example are listed in tables, so you can enter them in the software and get the same numbers as the book.</p><p>Most of Part II starts from the demonstration case that is loaded when the software opens. Below it is called the \"base case\":</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Inflow</td><td>Triangular, Qp = 0.120 m³/s, Tp = 30 min, Tr = 30 min</td></tr><tr><td>Pond</td><td>Constant plan area A = 180 m², floor Hfloor = 0, initial level H0 = 0</td></tr><tr><td>Outlet</td><td>Sharp-crested weir, Cw = 1.70, b = 0.20 m, crest P = 0</td></tr><tr><td>Drain-down condition</td><td>Td = 24 h, permitted residual 1 %</td></tr><tr><td>Design limits</td><td>Qallow = 0.080 m³/s, Hallow = 1.00 m</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>When the book says \"start from the base case\", first reset the inputs to the values in the table above. The easiest way is to open \"Start here → Book\", press \"Back to the base case\", and then press \"Run routing and evaluation\" in Module 4. Reloading the web page has the same effect (switch to English again if needed). Both ways clear unsaved changes, so save any case you want to keep as a project first.</p>","sec":[],"hasBody":true},{"id":"bk-c11","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c11\">Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c11-s1\">10.1 Principle</h3><p>After development, the ground surface is less permeable, so the same storm produces a larger and faster flood peak. A detention pond stores part of the water and releases it slowly, so the peak seen downstream becomes smaller and later.</p><p>A design usually has two limits.</p><ul><li>Allowable outflow Qallow: the largest outflow allowed by the downstream drainage system or by regulations.</li><li>Allowable water level Hallow: the upper limit of the water level in the pond, usually set by the pond depth, the freeboard or the surrounding ground levels.</li></ul><p>A design is \"good enough\" only when Qp,o ≤ Qallow and Hmax ≤ Hallow at the same time, and the event-added storage left in the pond after the required drain-down time Td does not exceed the declared percentage.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c11-s2\">10.2 Example: is the base case good enough?</h3><p>This example assumes Qallow = 0.080 m³/s and Hallow = 1.00 m (teaching assumption).</p><ol><li>Open PondGate and keep the base case (if you have changed any input, press \"Back to the base case\" first).</li><li>In Module 5, enter Qallow = 0.080 and Hallow = 1.00.</li><li>Go back to Module 4 and press \"Run routing and evaluation\".</li></ol><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"book/fig_en/demo_qchart.png\" alt=\"Inflow and outflow hydrographs of the base case\"><figcaption>Inflow and outflow hydrographs of the base case</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 10-1 shows the inflow and outflow hydrographs of the base case. The results page reads as follows.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th><th>Limit</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peak outflow Qp,o</td><td>0.0903 m³/s</td><td>Qallow = 0.0800 m³/s</td><td>Exceeds</td></tr><tr><td>Peak water level Hmax</td><td>0.413 m</td><td>Hallow = 1.000 m</td><td>Within</td></tr><tr><td>Peak ratio RQ</td><td>0.753</td><td>—</td><td>About 25 % peak reduction</td></tr><tr><td>Drain-down residual (Te + Td)</td><td>0.03 %</td><td>1.00 %</td><td>Within</td></tr><tr><td>Event-added detention volume ΔSmax</td><td>74.385 m³</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The \"Beginner check\" box says \"Not good enough\", because the peak outflow exceeds the allowable value. The water level still has a large margin. This means the pond is not too small; the outlet is too large and releases water too fast. Chapter 14 uses the same case to find a direction for improvement.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c11-s3\">10.3 Key points</h3><ul><li>To judge \"good enough\", read the outflow, the water level and the drain-down together. Reading only one of them can mislead you.</li><li>When one limit is exceeded and another has a large margin, the pond area and the outlet are usually not well balanced.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c11-s4\">10.4 Exercises</h3><ol><li>Change Qallow to 0.100 m³/s. Is the base case good enough?</li><li>Why does Qp,o exceed the limit although Hmax is only 0.413 m? Explain in one sentence.</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c11-s1","title":"10.1 Principle"},{"id":"bk-c11-s2","title":"10.2 Example: is the base case good enough?"},{"id":"bk-c11-s3","title":"10.3 Key points"},{"id":"bk-c11-s4","title":"10.4 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c12","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c12\">Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c12-s1\">11.1 Principle</h3><p>The change of the water volume in the pond equals the inflow minus the outflow:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">dS/dt = Qin(t) − Qout(H)</div><p>With a constant plan area, the storage is proportional to the water level:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">S(H) = A × (H − Hfloor)</div><p>Together these two equations are called \"level-pool routing\". It assumes that the water surface is level at every moment. It suits detention ponds with a large area, shallow water and low velocity.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c12-s2\">11.2 Hand calculation: inflow volume</h3><p>The volume of a triangular hydrograph is the area of the triangle:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">Vin = ½ × Qp × (Tp + Tr) = ½ × 0.120 × 3600 = 216 m³</div><p>The software computes an event-added detention volume ΔSmax = 74.385 m³, so the storage ratio is RS = 74.385 ÷ 216 = 0.344, the same as in the results table. In other words, at most about one third of the inflow volume was in the pond at the same time.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c12-s3\">11.3 An important property: the outflow peak lies on the falling limb of the inflow</h3><p>When the outflow depends only on the water level in the pond (free discharge), dS/dt = 0 at the moment of the highest level, so Qin = Qout. Therefore the outflow peak always lies on the falling limb of the inflow hydrograph.</p><p>Check with the base case: an independent program puts the outflow peak at t = 37.4 min, with Qout = 0.0903 m³/s. The inflow at that moment is:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">Qin = 0.120 × (60 − 37.4) ÷ 30 = 0.0904 m³/s</div><p>The two differ by less than 0.0001 m³/s; the difference comes from rounding the time to 0.1 min. In Figure 10-1 the highest point of the outflow curve touches the falling line of the inflow. This is the property at work.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c12-s4\">11.4 Key points</h3><ul><li>The moment of the highest water level is the moment of the largest outflow, and also the moment when Qin = Qout.</li><li>If another program shows an outflow peak that is not on the falling limb of the inflow, first check whether the outlet is affected by downstream conditions.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c12-s5\">11.5 Exercises</h3><ol><li>If Tp = Tr = 45 min and Qp is unchanged, what is the inflow volume?</li><li>With A = 180 m², how much water-level rise does ΔSmax = 74.385 m³ represent? Compare it with Hmax.</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c12-s1","title":"11.1 Principle"},{"id":"bk-c12-s2","title":"11.2 Hand calculation: inflow volume"},{"id":"bk-c12-s3","title":"11.3 An important property: the outflow peak lies on the falling limb of the inflow"},{"id":"bk-c12-s4","title":"11.4 Key points"},{"id":"bk-c12-s5","title":"11.5 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c13","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c13\">Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c13-s1\">12.1 Sharp-crested weir</h3><div class=\"bk-eq\">Qout = Cw × b × (H − P)^1.5</div><p>In the base case, Cw × b = 1.70 × 0.20 = 0.34. Table 12-1 lists the discharge at different heads.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Head H − P (m)</th><th>0.10</th><th>0.20</th><th>0.30</th><th>0.40</th><th>0.50</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weir flow Qout (m³/s)</td><td>0.0108</td><td>0.0304</td><td>0.0559</td><td>0.0860</td><td>0.1202</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The discharge grows with the 1.5 power of the head, so a small rise in level greatly increases the outflow.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c13-s2\">12.2 Rectangular opening</h3><p>PondGate computes a rectangular opening in two ranges. The opening height is a, the width is w = Ao ÷ a, and the head is y = H − invert elevation.</p><ul><li>The water has not risen above the top of the opening (y ≤ a): the opening acts as a weir of width w, Q = Cw × w × y^1.5.</li><li>The water is above the top of the opening (y &gt; a): the discharge starts from the weir flow at the top of the opening and adds the increase given by the orifice equation. The discharge is therefore continuous at the transition point and does not jump:</li></ul><div class=\"bk-eq\">Q = Cw × w × a^1.5 + Cd × w × a × [ √(2g(y − a/2)) − √(2g × a/2) ]</div><p>Take Ao = 0.010 m², a = 0.10 m, invert 0.05 m, Cw = 1.70 and Cd = 0.62 as an example. Table 12-2 compares the discharges of the weir and the opening.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Water level H (m)</th><th>0.15</th><th>0.30</th><th>0.50</th><th>0.75</th><th>1.00</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Weir b = 0.20 m (m³/s)</td><td>0.0198</td><td>0.0559</td><td>0.1202</td><td>0.2208</td><td>0.3400</td></tr><tr><td>Opening Ao = 0.010 m² (m³/s)</td><td>0.0054</td><td>0.0115</td><td>0.0166</td><td>0.0214</td><td>0.0253</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The discharge of the opening grows only with about the 0.5 power of the head. As the level rises, a weir releases water quickly, while an opening releases it slowly. This difference drives the design behaviour in Chapters 14 and 15.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c13-s3\">12.3 Coefficients and free discharge</h3><p>Cw = 1.70 and Cd = 0.62 are common preliminary values. The coefficients of a real structure depend on its shape, edges and approach velocity. A formal design should use calibrated values.</p><p>All these equations assume free discharge: the downstream water level is low enough not to back up into the outlet. If the outlet may be submerged from downstream, the equations no longer hold. This is why constrained screening requires the free-discharge evidence.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c13-s4\">12.4 Exercises</h3><ol><li>If the weir width is doubled, by what factor does the weir flow change at the same head?</li><li>When the water level at the opening rises from 0.50 m to 1.00 m, by what percentage does the discharge increase? And for the weir?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c13-s1","title":"12.1 Sharp-crested weir"},{"id":"bk-c13-s2","title":"12.2 Rectangular opening"},{"id":"bk-c13-s3","title":"12.3 Coefficients and free discharge"},{"id":"bk-c13-s4","title":"12.4 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c14","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c14\">Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c14-s1\">13.1 Principle</h3><p>The continuity equation has no general analytical solution, so it is integrated numerically step by step. PondGate uses the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method (RK4), which computes four slopes in each step:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">k1 = f(t, S)<br>k2 = f(t + Δt/2, S + Δt × k1/2)<br>k3 = f(t + Δt/2, S + Δt × k2/2)<br>k4 = f(t + Δt, S + Δt × k3)<br>S(next) = S + Δt/6 × (k1 + 2k2 + 2k3 + k4)</div><p>Here f(t, S) = Qin(t) − Qout(H(S)), and the PondGate step is Δt = 2 s.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c14-s2\">13.2 One step by hand</h3><p>Take the base case at t = 1800 s (the time of the inflow peak). At this moment S = 65.608 m³, H = 0.3645 m, Qin = 0.1200 m³/s and Qout = 0.0748 m³/s.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Slope</th><th>Value (m³/s)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>k1</td><td>0.045181</td></tr><tr><td>k2</td><td>0.045037</td></tr><tr><td>k3</td><td>0.045038</td></tr><tr><td>k4</td><td>0.044894</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Substituting gives S_next = 65.608 + 2 ÷ 6 × (0.045181 + 2 × 0.045037 + 2 × 0.045038 + 0.044894) = 65.699 m³, and the water level rises to 0.3650 m. The four slopes are very close, which shows that a 2-second step is very small for this case.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c14-s3\">13.3 Time-step sensitivity</h3><p>Table 13-1 compares results for different steps from an independent program. RK4 hardly changes for steps up to 60 s. The simple forward Euler method loses accuracy quickly as the step grows.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Method and step</th><th>Qp,o (m³/s)</th><th>Hmax (m)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>RK4, 2 s (PondGate)</td><td>0.09032</td><td>0.4132</td></tr><tr><td>RK4, 60 s</td><td>0.09028</td><td>0.4131</td></tr><tr><td>RK4, 300 s</td><td>0.08896</td><td>0.4091</td></tr><tr><td>Euler, 60 s</td><td>0.09179</td><td>0.4177</td></tr><tr><td>Euler, 300 s</td><td>0.09910</td><td>0.4396</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The peaks for large steps in the table are also affected by sampling: with a 300-second step, the result is seen only every 5 minutes.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c14-s4\">13.4 Mass-balance residual</h3><p>The mass-balance residual ε/Vin checks whether \"cumulative inflow − cumulative outflow\" equals the change in storage. For the base case the absolute residual is 1.259 × 10⁻¹⁰ m³, and the relative residual is shown as 0.000 %. Above 0.5 %, the results must not be used; check the inputs first.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c14-s5\">13.5 How the numbers in this book are checked</h3><p>Every example in Part II was run in the software by docs/book/run_examples.js, which read the results from the screen. docs/book/check_examples.py then recomputed them with an independently written RK4 program. The two agree on Qp,o, Hmax, ΔSmax and the drain-down residual in every case (Qp,o differs by less than 0.00005 m³/s and Hmax by less than 0.0005 m). When one number is changed on purpose, the check program reports an error.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c14-s6\">13.6 Exercises</h3><ol><li>Why are the outflow peaks for large steps too low? Hint: think about the sampling times.</li><li>If the mass-balance residual shows 2 %, which inputs should you check first?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c14-s1","title":"13.1 Principle"},{"id":"bk-c14-s2","title":"13.2 One step by hand"},{"id":"bk-c14-s3","title":"13.3 Time-step sensitivity"},{"id":"bk-c14-s4","title":"13.4 Mass-balance residual"},{"id":"bk-c14-s5","title":"13.5 How the numbers in this book are checked"},{"id":"bk-c14-s6","title":"13.6 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c15","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c15\">Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c15-s1\">14.1 Problem</h3><p>In the base case of Chapter 10 the outflow is too large (0.0903 m³/s &gt; 0.080 m³/s). There are two ways to adjust it: change the weir width b, or change the pond area A. This chapter changes one variable at a time and watches how the results change.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c15-s2\">14.2 Steps</h3><ol><li>Start from the base case (Start here → Book → press \"Back to the base case\", or reload the page).</li><li>In Module 3, change the weir width b to 0.10 m, press \"Run routing and evaluation\" and record the results.</li><li>Change b to 0.40 m, press \"Run routing and evaluation\" and record the results.</li><li>Press \"Back to the base case\" (b returns to 0.20 m). Then change the area A in Module 2 to 120 m² and press \"Run routing and evaluation\".</li><li>Change A to 360 m² and press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c15-s3\">14.3 Results</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Change</th><th>Qp,o (m³/s)</th><th>Hmax (m)</th><th>ΔSmax (m³)</th><th>RQ</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>b = 0.10 m</td><td>0.0732</td><td>0.570</td><td>102.635</td><td>0.610</td></tr><tr><td>Base case b = 0.20 m, A = 180 m²</td><td>0.0903</td><td>0.413</td><td>74.385</td><td>0.753</td></tr><tr><td>b = 0.40 m</td><td>0.1027</td><td>0.284</td><td>51.045</td><td>0.856</td></tr><tr><td>A = 120 m²</td><td>0.1015</td><td>0.447</td><td>53.590</td><td>0.846</td></tr><tr><td>A = 360 m²</td><td>0.0636</td><td>0.327</td><td>117.754</td><td>0.530</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"book/fig_en/weir_b010_qchart.png\" alt=\"Hydrographs with a weir width of 0.10 m\"><figcaption>Hydrographs with a weir width of 0.10 m</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 14-1 shows the hydrographs for b = 0.10 m. Compared with Figure 10-1, the outflow is flatter and later.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c15-s4\">14.4 Interpretation</h3><ul><li>A narrower weir: the outflow falls, but the water level rises. The water is \"held\" in the pond, so more detention volume is needed.</li><li>A larger area: both the outflow and the water level fall. The price is more land.</li><li>Both b = 0.10 m and A = 360 m² bring Qp,o below 0.080 m³/s. With the first, the level rises to 0.570 m; with the second, only to 0.327 m. Which is better depends on the land cost and the allowable level.</li></ul><p>This is the direction that the \"Beginner check\" box suggests: a larger pond area usually lowers both the peak water level and the peak outflow; a smaller outlet lowers the peak outflow but raises the peak water level.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c15-s5\">14.5 Exercises</h3><ol><li>Using the base case, find the largest weir width that brings Qp,o just below 0.080 m³/s (to 0.01 m).</li><li>If Hallow = 0.50 m, which designs in the table above also meet Qallow = 0.080 m³/s?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c15-s1","title":"14.1 Problem"},{"id":"bk-c15-s2","title":"14.2 Steps"},{"id":"bk-c15-s3","title":"14.3 Results"},{"id":"bk-c15-s4","title":"14.4 Interpretation"},{"id":"bk-c15-s5","title":"14.5 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c16","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c16\">Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c16-s1\">15.1 Problem</h3><p>Replace the outlet of the base case with a rectangular opening (opening height 0.10 m, invert 0.05 m, Cd = 0.62) and compare three opening areas.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c16-s2\">15.2 Steps</h3><ol><li>Start from the base case (press \"Back to the base case\"). In Module 3, untick \"Sharp-crested weir\" and tick \"Rectangular opening\".</li><li>Enter the opening area Ao as 0.005, 0.010 and 0.020 m² in turn, and press \"Run routing and evaluation\" after each one.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c16-s3\">15.3 Results</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Ao (m²)</th><th>Qp,o (m³/s)</th><th>Hmax (m)</th><th>ΔSmax (m³)</th><th>Storage left at Te + Td</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.005</td><td>0.0130</td><td>1.056</td><td>190.013</td><td>5.01 %</td></tr><tr><td>0.010</td><td>0.0243</td><td>0.936</td><td>168.476</td><td>5.41 %</td></tr><tr><td>0.020</td><td>0.0428</td><td>0.752</td><td>135.278</td><td>6.67 %</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"book/fig_en/ori_ao010_qchart.png\" alt=\"Hydrographs with an opening of 0.010 m²\"><figcaption>Hydrographs with an opening of 0.010 m²</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 15-1 shows the hydrographs for Ao = 0.010 m². The outflow is very flat and continues long after the event ends.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c16-s4\">15.4 Interpretation</h3><ul><li>All three openings keep the outflow peak far below 0.080 m³/s, so the peak reduction is strong.</li><li>But the water levels are all high. With Ao = 0.005 m² the level even exceeds the allowable 1.00 m.</li><li>The drain-down matters even more: after 24 hours, more than 5 % of the event-added storage is still in the pond, above the declared 1 %. The main reason is the opening invert of 0.05 m. The water below the opening, 0.05 m × 180 m² = 9 m³, can never flow out, and this is about 5 % of ΔSmax. In addition, the opening releases water very slowly at low levels (Chapter 12). The pond does not drain, so less volume is available when the next storm comes.</li></ul><p>This chapter shows that looking only at the outflow peak can lead to a design that does not drain. This is why PondGate treats the drain-down residual as a screening limit. The \"Beginner check\" also lists the drain-down residual as its third item, and all three openings show \"exceeds limit\" there.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c16-s5\">15.5 Exercises</h3><ol><li>When the opening area is doubled, by about what factor does Qp,o change? Why is it not exactly 2?</li><li>If the drain-down time Td is changed to 48 h, does the residual for Ao = 0.010 m² become larger or smaller? Check it in the software.</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c16-s1","title":"15.1 Problem"},{"id":"bk-c16-s2","title":"15.2 Steps"},{"id":"bk-c16-s3","title":"15.3 Results"},{"id":"bk-c16-s4","title":"15.4 Interpretation"},{"id":"bk-c16-s5","title":"15.5 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c17","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c17\">Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c17-s1\">16.1 Problem</h3><p>Three inflows all have a peak of 0.120 m³/s. The pond and weir are those of the base case; only the inflow shape changes.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Inflow</th><th>Settings</th><th>Inflow volume</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Short triangle (base case)</td><td>Tp = Tr = 30 min</td><td>216 m³</td></tr><tr><td>Long flat-topped</td><td>Rise 24, plateau 72, recession 24 min</td><td>691.2 m³</td></tr><tr><td>Long triangle</td><td>Tp = Tr = 96 min</td><td>691.2 m³</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"bk-c17-s2\">16.2 Steps</h3><ol><li>Long flat-topped: in Module 1, set \"Standard case\" to the long-duration event, keep the other defaults and press \"Run routing and evaluation\".</li><li>Long triangle: set the triangular event again, change both Tp and Tr to 96 min and press \"Run routing and evaluation\".</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c17-s3\">16.3 Results</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Inflow</th><th>Qp,o (m³/s)</th><th>RQ</th><th>Hmax (m)</th><th>ΔSmax (m³)</th><th>FPI95</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Short triangle</td><td>0.0903</td><td>0.753</td><td>0.413</td><td>74.385</td><td>0.006</td></tr><tr><td>Long flat-topped</td><td>0.1200</td><td>1.000</td><td>0.499</td><td>89.890</td><td>0.036</td></tr><tr><td>Long triangle</td><td>0.1123</td><td>0.936</td><td>0.478</td><td>86.018</td><td>0.011</td></tr></tbody></table></div><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"book/fig_en/long_qchart.png\" alt=\"Hydrographs of the long-duration event\"><figcaption>Hydrographs of the long-duration event</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 16-1 shows the hydrographs of the long flat-topped event. The plateau lasts 72 minutes. The pond fills during the plateau, and the outflow catches up with the inflow.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c17-s4\">16.4 Interpretation</h3><ul><li>The peaks are the same, but the results differ a lot. The long flat-topped event has RQ = 1.000, which means this pond gives no peak reduction at all.</li><li>The reason is the limited capacity of the pond. When the water keeps coming long enough, the pond fills to the equilibrium level where \"outflow equals inflow\", and the detention effect disappears.</li><li>The long-duration events have a larger FPI95, which means the top of the outflow is flat. The \"time of peak\" then has little meaning; use the median-volume time and the discharge-centroid time instead.</li></ul><p>So a design cannot rely on the peak discharge alone. For the same peak, check the most unfavourable duration and shape.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c17-s5\">16.5 Exercises</h3><ol><li>In the long flat-topped event, what is the water level when the outflow equals the inflow? Work it back from the weir equation in Chapter 12.</li><li>If the plateau time is changed to 36 minutes, how does RQ change?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c17-s1","title":"16.1 Problem"},{"id":"bk-c17-s2","title":"16.2 Steps"},{"id":"bk-c17-s3","title":"16.3 Results"},{"id":"bk-c17-s4","title":"16.4 Interpretation"},{"id":"bk-c17-s5","title":"16.5 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c18","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c18\">Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c18-s1\">17.1 Problem</h3><p>Real ponds often have sloped sides or irregular shapes, so the area changes with the level. In that case, describe the pond with a stage–storage table. This example uses the data of Table 17-1; all other conditions are the base case.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Water level H (m)</th><th>0</th><th>0.25</th><th>0.50</th><th>0.75</th><th>1.00</th><th>1.50</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Storage S (m³)</td><td>0</td><td>30</td><td>80</td><td>150</td><td>240</td><td>420</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>This table is also in the download package as ch17_irregular_stage_storage.csv.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c18-s2\">17.2 Steps</h3><ol><li>Start from the base case (press \"Back to the base case\"). In Module 2, set the storage representation to the tabulated stage–storage relation.</li><li>Paste Table 17-1 into the table field (one \"level, storage\" pair per line), or import the CSV file.</li><li>Press \"Run routing and evaluation\".</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c18-s3\">17.3 Results</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Pond shape</th><th>Qp,o (m³/s)</th><th>Hmax (m)</th><th>ΔSmax (m³)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constant area 180 m² (base case)</td><td>0.0903</td><td>0.413</td><td>74.385</td></tr><tr><td>Irregular pond (Table 17-1)</td><td>0.0912</td><td>0.416</td><td>63.161</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"bk-c18-s4\">17.4 Interpretation</h3><p>The irregular pond is smaller at low levels (the mean area between 0 and 0.25 m is only 120 m²). The water level rises faster, so the outlet starts to release a lot of water earlier. At 0.416 m the irregular pond holds only about 63 m³, while the constant-area pond holds about 75 m³. Less water stays in the pond and more is released, so Qp,o is slightly higher and the detention volume is about 11 m³ smaller, while the peak levels are almost the same.</p><p>When you use a stage–storage table, note the following:</p><ul><li>The first row is usually the pond floor (S = 0).</li><li>Routing does not extrapolate beyond the table. The highest level in the table must be above the highest possible water level; otherwise the result is invalid.</li><li>The table and the outlet elevations must use the same datum.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c18-s5\">17.5 Exercises</h3><ol><li>From Table 17-1, compute the mean area between 0.25 and 0.50 m.</li><li>What happens if the table only reaches H = 0.40 m?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c18-s1","title":"17.1 Problem"},{"id":"bk-c18-s2","title":"17.2 Steps"},{"id":"bk-c18-s3","title":"17.3 Results"},{"id":"bk-c18-s4","title":"17.4 Interpretation"},{"id":"bk-c18-s5","title":"17.5 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c19","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c19\">Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s1\">18.1 Problem</h3><p>In Chapter 14 you found a few feasible designs by manual trial and error. This chapter uses the constrained design screening of Module 5 instead, so the software searches the declared range automatically. The limits are the same as in Chapter 10: Qallow = 0.080 m³/s, Hallow = 1.00 m and a residual of no more than 1 % within 24 hours.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s2\">18.2 Preparation: free-discharge evidence</h3><p>The free-discharge evidence must be complete before constrained screening. This example is a teaching case, so the ticks and records are teaching assumptions.</p><ol><li>Start from the base case (Start here → Book → press \"Back to the base case\").</li><li>In Module 3, tick \"Free-discharge boundary confirmed\" and the four checks.</li><li>In the \"Free-discharge evidence record\", enter: Teaching assumption (practice only): a single outlet with free discharge, not affected by tailwater, backwater, submergence or operational control.</li><li>In the \"Screening-limit basis\", enter: Teaching assumption (practice only): Qallow and Hallow are example values set in this book.</li></ol><p>In a real project these two records must give the drawing or inspection number, the checker and the date, and the regulatory source of the limit values.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s3\">18.3 Example 1: minimum detention volume</h3><ol><li>In Module 5, enter Qallow = 0.080 and Hallow = 1.00, set the objective to \"Minimum event-added detention volume ΔSmax\", and keep the weir-width bounds at 0.05 to 1.00 m.</li><li>Press \"Run constrained design screening\".</li></ol><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"book/fig_en/screen_ok_optout.png\" alt=\"Screening result (minimum detention volume)\"><figcaption>Screening result (minimum detention volume)</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 18-1 shows the screening result. The software searches areas from 60 to 600 m² (on a 20 m² grid) and the declared weir-width range, and then runs 2 passes of local refinement.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plan area A</td><td>460 m²</td></tr><tr><td>Weir width b</td><td>0.53 m (the input field shows 0.525)</td></tr><tr><td>Event-added detention volume ΔSmax</td><td>92.0 m³</td></tr><tr><td>Peak outflow / allowable</td><td>0.0798 / 0.0800 m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Peak water level / allowable</td><td>0.200 / 1.000 m</td></tr><tr><td>Drain-down residual</td><td>0.07 / 1.00 %</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s4\">18.4 Example 2: minimum area</h3><p>Change the objective to \"Minimum plan area A\" and press \"Run constrained design screening\" again.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plan area A</td><td>100 m²</td></tr><tr><td>Weir width b</td><td>0.05 m (at the declared lower bound)</td></tr><tr><td>Event-added detention volume ΔSmax</td><td>94.6 m³</td></tr><tr><td>Peak outflow / allowable</td><td>0.0782 / 0.0800 m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Peak water level / allowable</td><td>0.946 / 1.000 m</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s5\">18.5 Reading the two results</h3><ul><li>Both designs meet all limits, but the trade-offs are completely different. The minimum-volume design has very shallow water (0.200 m) and needs a large area. The minimum-area design needs only 100 m², but the water depth is close to the limit (0.946 m).</li><li>In the minimum-area design the weir width stops at the lower bound of 0.05 m, and the screen marks it as a boundary case. This means the pond might be smaller still if a narrower weir were allowed. But a very narrow weir blocks easily, so the lower bound should follow the maintenance needs, not be relaxed to get a better number.</li><li>Both results are only conditional screening within the declared range. Neither is a global optimum or a final design.</li></ul><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s6\">18.6 Example 3: Blocked</h3><p>Make the limits very strict: Qallow = 0.040 m³/s and Hallow = 0.30 m. Set the objective back to minimum detention volume and press \"Run constrained design screening\" again.</p><p>The result says: \"No feasible screening case was found within the declared physical bounds and discrete screening domain. Dominant constraints observed: peak-outflow limit, maximum-water-level limit, drain-down residual limit.\"</p><p>This block is reasonable. To bring a peak of 0.120 m³/s down to 0.040 m³/s, the pond must store a lot of water. But the level must not exceed 0.30 m, and even the upper area bound of 600 m² is not enough.</p><p>The right way to respond:</p><ol><li>First check that the sources of Qallow and Hallow are correct. If they are, do not relax them just to get a solution.</li><li>Based on the dominant limits, consider adjustments with an engineering basis: for example, a larger area range if the land allows it, another outlet type, or several smaller facilities.</li><li>If a larger area is needed, use continuous optimization with a larger area range and check again.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c19-s7\">18.7 Exercises</h3><ol><li>In Example 1, Qp,o = 0.0798 m³/s, very close to 0.080. Why do screening results often lie close to a limit?</li><li>In Example 3, relax only Hallow to 1.00 m (Qallow stays at 0.040) and run it in the software. Is it still blocked?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c19-s1","title":"18.1 Problem"},{"id":"bk-c19-s2","title":"18.2 Preparation: free-discharge evidence"},{"id":"bk-c19-s3","title":"18.3 Example 1: minimum detention volume"},{"id":"bk-c19-s4","title":"18.4 Example 2: minimum area"},{"id":"bk-c19-s5","title":"18.5 Reading the two results"},{"id":"bk-c19-s6","title":"18.6 Example 3: Blocked"},{"id":"bk-c19-s7","title":"18.7 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c20","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c20\">Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM</h2><h3 id=\"bk-c20-s1\">19.1 Why cross-check</h3><p>Different programs use different numerical methods. If the inputs are exactly the same but the results differ a lot, at least one side has an error in its inputs, units or settings. A cross-check can find such errors, but it only shows \"numerical consistency\". It does not show that the results match field conditions.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c20-s2\">19.2 Built-in example: E-W1 A-005</h3><p>PondGate has a read-only verification case, E-W1 A-005. It is a laboratory-scale case with a single weir. The event lasts from 0 to 977.25 s, and the initial level is H0 = 0.07399 m. The stage–storage and outflow equations of the case are fixed on the page and do not change your normal routing inputs.</p><ol><li>On the \"Results, verification and records\" page, find the \"E-W1 A-005 external cross-check benchmark\".</li><li>Press \"Run E-W1 A-005 benchmark RK4\".</li><li>Then press \"Time-step sensitivity (0.125 / 0.250 / 0.500 s)\".</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c20-s3\">19.3 Comparison of three engines</h3><p>The three-engine table on the page compares the stored results of PondGate (DSS RK4, Δt = 0.25 s), EPA SWMM 5.2.4 and HEC-HMS 4.13 over a common window of 3,910 time points.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Engine</th><th>Peak outflow (m³/s)</th><th>Peak time (s)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>PondGate RK4</td><td>0.00613899</td><td>154.50</td></tr><tr><td>HEC-HMS 4.13</td><td>0.00613899</td><td>154.50</td></tr><tr><td>EPA SWMM 5.2.4</td><td>0.00614476</td><td>154.75</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Pair</th><th>Outflow RMSE (m³/s)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HEC-HMS and PondGate</td><td>8.57 × 10⁻⁹</td></tr><tr><td>HEC-HMS and SWMM</td><td>2.30 × 10⁻⁶</td></tr><tr><td>PondGate and SWMM</td><td>2.30 × 10⁻⁶</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The peaks of the three engines differ by less than 0.1 %. HEC-HMS uses the Modified Puls method. To match its one-minute computation interval, the case uses a volume-conserving conversion of time and discharge: one HEC minute stands for 0.25 s of the original record, the HEC discharge is 1/240 of the original discharge, and the output is converted back to the original scale.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c20-s4\">19.4 Time-step sensitivity</h3><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>RK4 step</th><th>Maximum outflow (m³/s)</th><th>Mass residual (m³)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.125 s</td><td>6.13899 × 10⁻³</td><td>2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰</td></tr><tr><td>0.250 s</td><td>6.13899 × 10⁻³</td><td>9.98 × 10⁻¹⁰</td></tr><tr><td>0.500 s</td><td>6.13893 × 10⁻³</td><td>−7.93 × 10⁻⁵</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>When the step grows from 0.125 s to 0.500 s, the maximum outflow changes by only about 0.001 %, so a 0.25-second step is enough.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c20-s5\">19.5 Compare your own case</h3><ol><li>Build the same pond in HEC-HMS or SWMM: the same inflow, initial level, stage–storage relation, outlet equation and coefficients.</li><li>Save the actual simulation results of the external model as a CSV. The first row holds the column names. The required columns are time_s (or time, t) and outflow_m3_s (or qout, qo); a full comparison also needs incremental_storage_m3. Press \"Download external-output CSV template\" for an example of the format.</li><li>In the \"External-model cross check\", choose the reference hydrograph (the current routing result or one of the public benchmarks A-005 to D-005) and the external model, import the CSV and press \"Compare external result\".</li></ol><p>Common causes of disagreement are different units (L/s and m³/s), different time bases (minutes and seconds), different datums and different coefficients in the external model.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c20-s6\">19.6 Exercises</h3><ol><li>Why is the difference between HEC-HMS and PondGate much smaller than that between SWMM and PondGate? Hint: look at the data and methods each one uses.</li><li>If the outflow peak of the external model is 240 times larger than that of PondGate, what is the most likely error?</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c20-s1","title":"19.1 Why cross-check"},{"id":"bk-c20-s2","title":"19.2 Built-in example: E-W1 A-005"},{"id":"bk-c20-s3","title":"19.3 Comparison of three engines"},{"id":"bk-c20-s4","title":"19.4 Time-step sensitivity"},{"id":"bk-c20-s5","title":"19.5 Compare your own case"},{"id":"bk-c20-s6","title":"19.6 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c21","part":"Part II  Worked Examples","title":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c21\">Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site</h2><p>This chapter brings the earlier chapters together in one complete check. The site and the numbers are a fictitious teaching case.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s1\">20.1 Case conditions</h3><p>The post-development design inflow hydrograph of a development site is given in Table 20-1 (ch20_site_inflow.csv in the download package).</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Time (min)</th><th>0</th><th>10</th><th>20</th><th>30</th><th>40</th><th>50</th><th>60</th><th>80</th><th>100</th><th>120</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Discharge (m³/s)</td><td>0</td><td>0.03</td><td>0.09</td><td>0.21</td><td>0.25</td><td>0.18</td><td>0.12</td><td>0.06</td><td>0.025</td><td>0</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Design limits (teaching assumption): Qallow = 0.15 m³/s, Hallow = 0.80 m, and a residual of no more than 1 % within 24 hours. The outlet is a sharp-crested weir with Cw = 1.70 and the crest at the pond floor.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s2\">20.2 Step 1: compute the inflow volume by hand</h3><p>Use the trapezoidal rule: multiply the mean discharge of each pair of neighbouring points by the time interval, and add them up:</p><div class=\"bk-eq\">Vin = Σ (Qi + Qi+1) ÷ 2 × Δti = 666 m³</div><p>You can use this number to check whether the RS values later are reasonable.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s3\">20.3 Step 2: enter the hydrograph and try a design</h3><ol><li>In Module 1, set \"Standard case\" to the user-defined event and the inflow shape to imported time-discharge pairs. Paste Table 20-1 (one \"time, discharge\" pair per line).</li><li>In Module 2, enter A = 300 m²; in Module 3, enter the weir width b = 0.30 m.</li><li>In Module 5, enter Qallow = 0.15 and Hallow = 0.80.</li><li>Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" and read the \"Beginner check\".</li></ol><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Trial</th><th>A (m²)</th><th>b (m)</th><th>Qp,o (m³/s)</th><th>Hmax (m)</th><th>ΔSmax (m³)</th><th>Reading</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>300</td><td>0.30</td><td>0.2037</td><td>0.542</td><td>162.703</td><td>Outflow exceeds</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>500</td><td>0.30</td><td>0.1662</td><td>0.474</td><td>236.763</td><td>Outflow still exceeds</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For trial 1, RS = 162.703 ÷ 666 = 0.244, the same as in the results table. A larger area lowers the outflow, but not enough, while the water level still has a large margin. From the lesson of Chapter 14, the weir should be made narrower at the same time.</p><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s4\">20.4 Step 3: constrained screening</h3><ol><li>Complete the free-discharge evidence (teaching assumption, as in Chapter 18).</li><li>In Module 5, set the weir-width bounds to 0.10 to 0.60 m and the objective to \"Minimum event-added detention volume ΔSmax\".</li><li>Press \"Run constrained design screening\".</li></ol><figure class=\"bk-fig\"><img data-bk-src=\"book/fig_en/site_screen_optout.png\" alt=\"Screening result of the capstone project\"><figcaption>Screening result of the capstone project</figcaption></figure><p>Figure 20-1 shows the screening result.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Plan area A</td><td>520 m²</td></tr><tr><td>Weir width b</td><td>0.23 m (the input field shows 0.225)</td></tr><tr><td>Event-added detention volume ΔSmax</td><td>274.5 m³</td></tr><tr><td>Peak outflow / allowable</td><td>0.1467 / 0.1500 m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Peak water level / allowable</td><td>0.528 / 0.800 m</td></tr><tr><td>Drain-down residual</td><td>0.17 / 1.00 %</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s5\">20.5 Step 4: check and record</h3><ol><li>Check that the mass-balance residual is close to 0 %.</li><li>Use the property from Chapter 11: the outflow peak should lie on the falling limb of the inflow.</li><li>Press \"Download reproducible decision-support report\" and file the report together with Table 20-1 and the basis of the limits.</li><li>Readers who are signed in can save this case as a project and share it with the colleague who reviews it.</li></ol><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s6\">20.6 Step 5: write the conclusion</h3><p>A good conclusion gives the source of the inputs, the source of the limits, the selected sizes, the comparison with the limits and the scope of application. For example:</p><p>\"For the design inflow of Table 20-1, Qallow = 0.15 m³/s and Hallow = 0.80 m, a detention pond with an area of 520 m² and a weir width of 0.23 m gives a peak outflow of 0.1467 m³/s, a peak water level of 0.528 m and a drain-down residual of 0.17 %, all within the limits. This result assumes free discharge and fixed coefficients. The freeboard, structural safety and blockage risk must be checked separately.\"</p><h3 id=\"bk-c21-s7\">20.7 Exercises</h3><ol><li>If the site allows a pond of only 400 m², screen again with the minimum-area objective and see whether a feasible case exists.</li><li>Write a short conclusion that explains why the pond size cannot be decided from the peak inflow of 0.25 m³/s alone.</li></ol>","sec":[{"id":"bk-c21-s1","title":"20.1 Case conditions"},{"id":"bk-c21-s2","title":"20.2 Step 1: compute the inflow volume by hand"},{"id":"bk-c21-s3","title":"20.3 Step 2: enter the hydrograph and try a design"},{"id":"bk-c21-s4","title":"20.4 Step 3: constrained screening"},{"id":"bk-c21-s5","title":"20.5 Step 4: check and record"},{"id":"bk-c21-s6","title":"20.6 Step 5: write the conclusion"},{"id":"bk-c21-s7","title":"20.7 Exercises"}]},{"id":"bk-c22","part":"Appendices","title":"Appendix A  Terms and symbols","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c22\">Appendix A  Terms and symbols</h2><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Symbol</th><th>Term</th><th>Chinese term</th><th>Unit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Qp, Qp,i</td><td>Peak inflow</td><td>入流尖峰</td><td>m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Qp,o</td><td>Peak outflow</td><td>出流尖峰</td><td>m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Tp, Tr</td><td>Time to peak, recession time</td><td>洪峰到達時間、退水時間</td><td>min</td></tr><tr><td>T, Te</td><td>Event duration, event end</td><td>總事件延時、事件結束時間</td><td>min</td></tr><tr><td>Vin</td><td>Inflow volume</td><td>入流體積</td><td>m³</td></tr><tr><td>S(H)</td><td>Stage–storage relation</td><td>水位—蓄水量關係</td><td>m³</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Plan area</td><td>平面面積</td><td>m²</td></tr><tr><td>Hfloor</td><td>Floor elevation</td><td>池底高程</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>H0</td><td>Initial level</td><td>初始水位</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>Hmax</td><td>Peak water level</td><td>最高水位</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>ΔSmax</td><td>Event-added detention volume</td><td>事件新增滯洪量</td><td>m³</td></tr><tr><td>Cw</td><td>Weir coefficient</td><td>堰流係數</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>b</td><td>Weir width</td><td>堰寬</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>P</td><td>Crest elevation</td><td>堰頂高程</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>Cd</td><td>Discharge coefficient</td><td>流量係數</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>Ao</td><td>Opening area</td><td>開口面積</td><td>m²</td></tr><tr><td>RQ</td><td>Peak ratio Qp,o ÷ Qp,i</td><td>尖峰比</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>RS</td><td>Storage ratio ΔSmax ÷ Vin</td><td>蓄水比</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>RT</td><td>Normalized lag</td><td>無因次遲滯</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>FPI95</td><td>Near-peak flatness index</td><td>近洪峰平坦指標</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>ε/Vin</td><td>Mass-balance residual</td><td>質量平衡殘差</td><td>%</td></tr><tr><td>Qallow</td><td>Allowable peak outflow</td><td>容許出流尖峰</td><td>m³/s</td></tr><tr><td>Hallow</td><td>Allowable water level</td><td>容許水位</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>Td</td><td>Maximum drain-down time</td><td>最長排空時間</td><td>h</td></tr><tr><td>Href</td><td>Reference level</td><td>參考水位</td><td>m</td></tr><tr><td>Δt</td><td>Time step</td><td>時間步長</td><td>s</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>For fuller explanations of the terms, see \"Start here → Glossary\" in the software.</p>","sec":[]},{"id":"bk-c23","part":"Appendices","title":"Appendix B  Error messages and what to do","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c23\">Appendix B  Error messages and what to do</h2><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Message or situation on screen</th><th>Meaning</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The run button is grey, with a red list above it</td><td>A field is unreasonable</td><td>Click a field name in the list and fix the field outlined in red</td></tr><tr><td>\"Screening not yet run\"</td><td>Only normal routing was done</td><td>For normal routing read the \"Beginner check\"; to screen, go to Module 5</td></tr><tr><td>Module 5 lists missing conditions</td><td>The conditions for constrained screening are not met</td><td>Complete what the list says to check and to enter</td></tr><tr><td>\"No feasible screening case was found …\"</td><td>No feasible design within the bounds</td><td>Adjust according to the dominant limits; see Chapter 18</td></tr><tr><td>Results are marked out of date</td><td>Inputs changed after the run</td><td>Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again</td></tr><tr><td>Mass-balance residual above 0.5 %</td><td>A numerical problem</td><td>Check the units, the datum and the range of the stage–storage table</td></tr><tr><td>Importing time–discharge pairs fails</td><td>Format error</td><td>The first time is 0, times increase, discharges are not negative, at least two lines</td></tr><tr><td>No results after opening a project</td><td>A project stores only the inputs</td><td>Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" in Module 4</td></tr><tr><td>A viewer cannot save</td><td>Role setting</td><td>Ask the company administrator to change the role, or ask an engineer to save</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","sec":[]},{"id":"bk-c24","part":"Appendices","title":"Appendix C  Answers to the exercises","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c24\">Appendix C  Answers to the exercises</h2><p>All numerical answers were obtained by running this version of the software and were checked with an independent program. For explanation questions, the key points are given.</p><div class=\"bk-tw\"><table class=\"bk-t\"><thead><tr><th>Exercise</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>10.1</td><td>Good enough (preliminary reading). Qp,o = 0.0903 ≤ 0.100 m³/s, Hmax = 0.413 ≤ 1.00 m and the drain-down residual 0.03 % ≤ 1 %. The beginner check says \"Preliminary reading: all three limits are met\".</td></tr><tr><td>10.2</td><td>A 0.20 m weir releases water too fast. The water flows away before it is stored, so the capacity of the pond is not fully used.</td></tr><tr><td>11.1</td><td>Vin = ½ × 0.120 × 5400 = 324 m³.</td></tr><tr><td>11.2</td><td>74.385 ÷ 180 = 0.413 m, equal to Hmax, because the floor and the initial level are both 0.</td></tr><tr><td>12.1</td><td>2 times. Weir flow is proportional to the weir width.</td></tr><tr><td>12.2</td><td>The opening rises from 0.0166 to 0.0253 m³/s, about 52 %. The weir rises from 0.1202 to 0.3400 m³/s, about 183 %.</td></tr><tr><td>13.1</td><td>A large step samples only at the end of each step, so it can miss the true peak. A larger step also gives a larger numerical error.</td></tr><tr><td>13.2</td><td>The units (L/s and m³/s, minutes and seconds), the datum, whether the stage–storage table covers the highest level, and any extreme or unreasonable input.</td></tr><tr><td>14.1</td><td>With b = 0.12 m, Qp,o = 0.0780 m³/s (Hmax = 0.527 m). With b = 0.13 m, Qp,o = 0.0801 m³/s, which exceeds the limit. So the largest weir width is 0.12 m.</td></tr><tr><td>14.2</td><td>Only A = 360 m² (Qp,o = 0.0636 m³/s, Hmax = 0.327 m). For b = 0.10 m, Hmax = 0.570 m, which exceeds 0.50 m.</td></tr><tr><td>15.1</td><td>It changes from 0.0243 to 0.0428 m³/s, about 1.76 times. With a larger opening the water level in the pond is lower, so the head is smaller, and the discharge of an opening grows only with about the 0.5 power of the head.</td></tr><tr><td>15.2</td><td>It becomes only slightly smaller: from 5.41 % to 5.36 %. The 9 m³ below the opening invert can never drain, so a longer drain-down time still cannot meet the 1 % limit.</td></tr><tr><td>16.1</td><td>0.120 = 0.34 × H^1.5, so H = (0.120 ÷ 0.34)^(2/3) = 0.499 m, exactly the Hmax of the software.</td></tr><tr><td>16.2</td><td>Qp,o = 0.1191 m³/s, RQ = 0.993, Hmax = 0.497 m. Even with half the plateau, the peak reduction is still less than 1 %, because 36 minutes is still long enough for the pond to approach the equilibrium level.</td></tr><tr><td>17.1</td><td>(80 − 30) ÷ 0.25 = 200 m².</td></tr><tr><td>17.2</td><td>If the peak level goes beyond the table, routing does not extrapolate, the S(H) boundary check shows a block and the result must not be used. Add data for higher levels.</td></tr><tr><td>18.1</td><td>The objective is the minimum detention volume. A larger outflow means less water must be stored, so the best point pushes the outflow up to just below Qallow.</td></tr><tr><td>18.2</td><td>It is not blocked. Screening finds A = 260 m² and b = 0.05 m (at the lower bound), with Qp,o = 0.0393 m³/s, Hmax = 0.598 m and a drain-down residual of 0.70 %. The block in Example 3 came mainly from Hallow = 0.30 m; but limits may only be changed on a regulatory or design basis.</td></tr><tr><td>19.1</td><td>HEC-HMS uses the same stage–storage and outflow tables as PondGate, so the difference comes only from the numerical method. SWMM uses its own storage curve and outlet representation, so the difference is larger, but the peaks still differ by less than 0.1 %.</td></tr><tr><td>19.2</td><td>Most likely the time and discharge scale conversion (laboratory scale factor 240) was not applied. Check the comparison-scale setting and the units of the external model.</td></tr><tr><td>20.1</td><td>Yes. The minimum-area objective selects A = 380 m² and b = 0.12 m (the input field shows 0.123), with Qp,o = 0.1392 m³/s, Hmax = 0.761 m and a drain-down residual of 0.20 %. With a smaller area, the water level is closer to the 0.80 m limit.</td></tr><tr><td>20.2</td><td>Key point: for the same peak, a different inflow duration and shape give a different inflow volume (Chapter 16). In this case the inflow volume is 666 m³, and the storage needed depends on both the volume and the release capacity of the outlet.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>","sec":[]},{"id":"bk-c25","part":"Appendices","title":"Appendix D  References and software citation","html":"<h2 id=\"bk-c25\">Appendix D  References and software citation</h2><ul><li>PondGate source code: GitHub hongyaoming-cloud/detention-pond-dss (MIT licence).</li><li>PondGate v1.0.0: DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23296722.</li><li>PondGate v1.0.1: DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23298538.</li><li>PondGate v1.0.2: DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23299011.</li><li>PondGate, all versions (concept DOI): 10.5281/zenodo.23296721, which resolves to the latest version.</li><li>U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, HEC-HMS 4.13 user's manual (Modified Puls routing).</li><li>U.S. EPA, Storm Water Management Model (SWMM) 5.2 user's manual.</li></ul><p>Suggested citation: Hong, Y.-M. PondGate: open-source detention-pond routing software (version 1.3.4). Zenodo. Use the version DOI shown on the Zenodo page. Related journal papers will be added to a later edition after publication.</p>","sec":[]}],"index":[{"id":"bk-c1","title":"Preface","text":"This book is the handbook of PondGate. It has two parts. Part I is the user manual. It follows the order of the software screens and explains what to enter, what to press and what to read in each module. First-time users can start with Chapter 4, \"Five-minute start\", and look up the other chapters when needed. Part II is a set of worked examples. It starts from the hydraulic principles of detention ponds. It then uses the public benchmark cases built into the software to work step by step through routing, interpretation, design screening and cross-checks with external models. Each chapter has four parts: principle, step-by-step example, check and exercises. Answers to the exercises are in Appendix C.","chapter":"Preface"},{"id":"bk-c1-s1","title":"Scope of this book","chapter":"Preface","text":"PondGate computes the water level, storage and outflow of one detention pond under a known inflow hydrograph. It does not compute rainfall–runoff, channel routing, long-term continuous simulation or ponds in series. Every result in this book holds only under the declared inputs and assumptions. Neither the software nor this book replaces design codes, site investigation or the judgement of a professional engineer. Every tick and record marked \"teaching assumption\" is for practice only and must not be used in real projects."},{"id":"bk-c1-s2","title":"Where the numbers come from","chapter":"Preface","text":"Every result number in Part II was read from this version of the software after a real run. An independent small program then recomputed it for checking. If you get a different number with the same software version, first check that your inputs are exactly the same as in the book."},{"id":"bk-c1-s3","title":"Version and citation","chapter":"Preface","text":"This English edition matches PondGate v1.3.4. The screenshots were taken in the English interface. The source code is public on GitHub (hongyaoming-cloud/detention-pond-dss), and the Zenodo DOIs of each version are listed in Appendix D. Author: Yao-Ming Hong, Professor, Department of Natural Resources and Environmental Studies, National Dong Hwa University, Hualien, Taiwan."},{"id":"bk-c2","title":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do","text":"PondGate is detention-pond routing software that runs in a web browser. For one detention pond under a known inflow hydrograph, it computes how the water level, storage and outflow change over time. It keeps the inputs, method and results of every run as a traceable record.","chapter":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do"},{"id":"bk-c2-s1","title":"1.1 The method in one sentence","chapter":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do","text":"The software solves the level-pool (storage continuity) equation: dS/dt = Qin(t) − Qout(H) Here S is the storage in the pond, H is the water level, Qin is the inflow and Qout is the outlet discharge. The equation is solved with the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method at a fixed step (RK4, Δt = 2 s). Chapters 11 to 13 explain the principle and show hand checks."},{"id":"bk-c2-s2","title":"1.2 What it can do","chapter":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do","text":"Inflow–outflow routing of a single detention pond, giving the peak reduction, the maximum water level and the event-added detention volume. \"Constrained design screening\" and \"continuous optimization\" under a declared allowable outflow and allowable level, to find feasible pond areas and outlet sizes. Download of a traceable report with the inputs, results and scope of application. Numerical comparison with HEC-HMS and SWMM results. After signing in, saving a case as a project and sharing it with colleagues in the same company (Chapter 2)."},{"id":"bk-c2-s3","title":"1.3 What it cannot do","chapter":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do","text":"Rainfall–runoff calculation: the user must supply the inflow hydrograph. Channel routing, long-term continuous simulation or ponds in series. Field calibration: agreement with other models is called \"numerical consistency\" only. Engineering approval: results are not a final design. Engineering responsibility stays with qualified professionals."},{"id":"bk-c2-s4","title":"1.4 The software will not say \"pass\" for you","chapter":"Chapter 1  What PondGate can and cannot do","text":"When no feasible design exists within the current limits and search bounds, PondGate shows \"Blocked\". It lists the dominant limits and the directions for revision, and it does not show \"pass\". This is deliberate: it stops users from getting a result that looks acceptable when the evidence is not enough. Chapter 18 uses an example to show how to read a blocked result."},{"id":"bk-c3","title":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"You must sign in to use the official PondGate website. Your company administrator creates your account. After signing in, you have all calculation functions, and you can also save a case as a project, continue it later and share it with colleagues in the same company. A teaching or demonstration version without sign-in has the same calculation functions but no project storage.","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects"},{"id":"bk-c3-s1","title":"2.1 Four roles","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"Role Can do Cannot do System administrator Add companies and accounts for each company Open company projects Company administrator Manage accounts of their own company and view all company projects Manage other companies Engineer Create and save their own projects and share them with colleagues Change colleagues' projects Viewer Open projects that colleagues have shared Save The system administrator cannot open company projects on purpose. This keeps the engineering data of each company inside that company."},{"id":"bk-c3-s2","title":"2.2 First sign-in","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"Press \"Sign in\" at the top right of the screen. Enter the user name and initial password that your administrator gave you. The system asks you to change to your own password at once. The new password needs at least 8 characters and must be entered twice. If you forget your password, ask your company administrator to reset it. The system does not send passwords."},{"id":"bk-c3-s3","title":"2.3 Order for creating accounts","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"The system administrator first adds the company. The system administrator creates one \"company administrator\" account for that company. The company administrator signs in and creates the engineer and viewer accounts. When creating an account, you can press \"Random password\" and then give the password to your colleague in private."},{"id":"bk-c3-s4","title":"2.4 Save and open projects","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"Engineers and company administrators can press \"Save / open projects\" to save all current inputs as a new project, with a name and a note. If you tick \"Share with my organisation (read-only for others)\", colleagues can open the project but can only view it, not change it. The current project name is shown at the top of the screen. When \"● unsaved changes\" appears, remember to press \"Save\". After you open a project, results do not appear automatically. Go to Module 4 and press \"Run routing and evaluation\" to compute again. This makes sure that the results always come from the current software version."},{"id":"bk-c3-s5","title":"2.5 Recycle bin and activity log","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"A deleted project only moves to the recycle bin; it is not really removed. Press \"Restore\" in the recycle bin to get it back. A company administrator can see the latest 300 actions under \"Accounts\", for example who created, shared or deleted which project and when."},{"id":"bk-c3-s6","title":"2.6 Where the data are stored","chapter":"Chapter 2  Accounts, companies and projects","text":"All calculations run inside your browser, and no data are sent during a calculation. Only when you press \"Save\" are the input values of the project stored in the PondGate cloud database (Cloudflare). Passwords are kept only as encrypted hashes, and nobody (not even the system administrator) can see your password."},{"id":"bk-c4","title":"Chapter 3  Screen tour","text":"Screen layout of PondGate Figure 3-1 shows the four main areas of PondGate. Tab bar: \"Start here\", \"Setup and routing\", \"Constrained screening\", \"Results, verification and records\" and \"Teaching and internal tests\". Language button: when it shows \"中文\", the interface is in English. \"? Help / AI assistant\" button, available at any time. Work area: the content of the current page.","chapter":"Chapter 3  Screen tour"},{"id":"bk-c4-s1","title":"3.1 The \"Start here\" help centre","chapter":"Chapter 3  Screen tour","text":"The \"Start here\" page Figure 3-2 shows the \"Start here\" page. The tabs at the top are, in order: Start here, Guided lessons, User manual, Full step-by-step guide, Book (this book), Tutorial videos, Glossary and AI assistant. The \"Next step\" box changes as you work and tells you what to do now. \"Guided lessons\" has 7 lessons from easy to harder. The system ticks each step when you finish it. The \"?\" next to a field name explains the term."},{"id":"bk-c4-s2","title":"3.2 Tips","chapter":"Chapter 3  Screen tour","text":"Each time you press a button, a message at the bottom right explains what just happened. Switching the language does not change the input values. If the inputs changed after the last run, switching the language reruns the calculation with the current inputs, and the screen warns you first. The software also works on a phone. The tab bar then moves to the top of the screen."},{"id":"bk-c5","title":"Chapter 4  Five-minute start","text":"This chapter uses the \"triangular event\" demonstration case, which is loaded when the software opens, to complete your first routing run. The demonstration case is for practice only and does not stand for any real project. Open PondGate and switch to English if needed. Check that the tab bar is on \"Setup and routing\". Scroll down to Module 4 at the bottom of the page and press \"Run routing and evaluation\". The page switches to \"Results, verification and records\". In the \"Beginner check\" box above the results, read what the system says about Hmax and Qp,o. In the results table, first check that the \"Mass-balance residual ε/Vin\" is close to 0 %. Then read the peak inflow, the peak outflow and the peak water level. Press \"Download reproducible decision-support report\" to save the record of this run to your computer. Inflow and routed outflow hydrographs Figure 4-1 shows the inflow and outflow hydrographs of the demonstration case. The outflow peak is lower than the inflow peak and comes later. This is the \"peak reduction\" and \"delay\" effect of a detention pond. Table 4-1 lists the numbers you should see. If the numbers are the same, you did it right. Item Value you should see Peak inflow Qp,i 0.1200 m³/s Peak outflow Qp,o 0.0903 m³/s Peak water level Hmax 0.413 m Mass-balance residual ε/Vin 0.000 % Beginner check Not good enough The default allowable outflow Qallow of the demonstration case is 0.080 m³/s. The peak outflow of 0.0903 m³/s is above this value, so the \"Beginner check\" says \"Not good enough\". This is the expected result, not an operating error. Chapters 10 and 14 explain the reason and how to improve the design. There are two ways to reset all inputs to the demonstration case. First, open \"Start here → Book\" and press \"Back to the base case\". Second, reload the web page. Both clear any unsaved changes. After a reload, switch to English again if the page opens in Chinese. Readers who want to follow every step with a screenshot can open \"Start here → Full step-by-step guide\", Chapter 3.","chapter":"Chapter 4  Five-minute start"},{"id":"bk-c6","title":"Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet"},{"id":"bk-c6-s1","title":"5.1 Module 1: inflow boundary","chapter":"Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet","text":"Module 1 inflow boundary Figure 5-1 shows Module 1. The inflow can be described in three ways. Triangular event: enter the peak inflow Qp, the time to peak Tp and the recession time Tr. Long-duration event: enter the rise, plateau and recession times to form a trapezoidal hydrograph. Imported time–discharge pairs: one \"time (min), discharge (m³/s)\" pair per line. The first time must be 0, times must increase, discharges must not be negative, and at least two lines are needed. The total event duration T is the sum of the parts and cannot be entered directly. Remember that the peak discharge alone is not enough: the duration and shape of the hydrograph also change the detention volume needed (Chapter 16 shows an example)."},{"id":"bk-c6-s2","title":"5.2 Module 2: stage–storage relation","chapter":"Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet","text":"Module 2 storage–water-level state Figure 5-2 shows Module 2. Storage can be described in two ways. Constant plan area: S(H) = A × (H − Hfloor). Only the area A is needed. Tabulated stage–storage relation: import a two-column .csv or .txt file, with the water level (m) in the first column and the storage (m³) in the second. Routing does not extrapolate beyond the table. The floor elevation Hfloor, the initial level H0 and all outlet elevations must use the same datum. With a relative datum, set Hfloor = 0."},{"id":"bk-c6-s3","title":"5.3 Module 3: outlet hydraulics and free-discharge evidence","chapter":"Chapter 5  Modules 1 to 3: describe the inflow, the pond and the outlet","text":"Module 3 outlet hydraulics and free-discharge evidence Figure 5-3 shows Module 3. Only two outlet types can enter formal constrained screening. Sharp-crested weir: Qout = Cw × b × (H − P)^1.5. Enter the weir coefficient Cw, the weir width b and the crest elevation P. Rectangular opening: enter the discharge coefficient Cd, the opening area Ao, the opening height and the invert elevation. Before the water rises above the top of the opening, the opening acts like a narrow weir. After that, the discharge increases with head according to the orifice equation. Chapter 12 gives the full equations. Risers, circular pipes, stage-dependent coefficient tables and combined outlets (weir and opening together) are for \"research inspection\". You can route them and read the results, but constrained design screening is locked. You can use the default coefficients (Cw = 1.70, Cd = 0.62) or enter calibrated site values. The defaults are suitable only for preliminary screening. The free-discharge evidence consists of one main tick, four checks (tailwater, backwater and pipe friction, submergence, operational control) and two text records. Tick them only when you really have drawings, site checks or calculations to support them. The software does not verify these conditions for you."},{"id":"bk-c7","title":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results"},{"id":"bk-c7-s1","title":"6.1 Run","chapter":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results","text":"Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" in Module 4 to start the calculation. If the button is grey, a field in Modules 1 to 3 has an unreasonable value. The fields to fix are listed above the button. Click one to jump to it; it is outlined in red. Common causes are: Qp less than or equal to 0, an area of 0, a weir width or coefficient of 0, an outlet elevation below the floor, or no active outlet."},{"id":"bk-c7-s2","title":"6.2 Beginner check","chapter":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results","text":"The \"Beginner check\" box at the top of the results page compares three results directly with the limits you declared: the peak water level Hmax with the allowable level Hallow, the peak outflow Qp,o with the allowable outflow Qallow, and the residual after the drain-down time Td with the allowable residual percentage. Green: all three are below their limits. Yellow: a value equals its limit and there is no safety margin. Treat it with caution. Red: a limit is exceeded. The box suggests a direction: a larger pond area usually lowers both the peak water level and the peak outflow; a smaller outlet lowers the peak outflow but raises the peak water level. The beginner check only compares readings. It is not an engineering approval."},{"id":"bk-c7-s3","title":"6.3 Order for reading the results table","chapter":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results","text":"Results table Figure 6-1 shows the results table. Read it in this order. Mass-balance residual ε/Vin: it should be close to 0 %. Above 0.5 %, the system asks you to solve the numerical problem first, and the results must not be used. Peak outflow Qp,o and peak ratio RQ = Qp,o ÷ Qp,i: a smaller RQ means more peak reduction. Peak water level Hmax and event-added detention volume ΔSmax: compare them with the allowable level and the freeboard. Near-peak flatness FPI95: a large value means a very flat peak. A single peak time is then not reliable; use the volume-based timing measures instead."},{"id":"bk-c7-s4","title":"6.4 When the inputs change, the results expire","chapter":"Chapter 6  Modules 4 and 6: run the routing and read the results","text":"If an input changes after a run, the screen marks the results as out of date. Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again before you interpret them. Ticking the free-discharge evidence or filling in the record fields does not make the routing results expire, because these fields do not change the computed numbers."},{"id":"bk-c8","title":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization","text":"Constrained design screening answers this question: within the limits and search range I give, is there a pond area and outlet size that meets all limits at the same time?","chapter":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization"},{"id":"bk-c8-s1","title":"7.1 When screening is available","chapter":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization","text":"Constrained screening is available only when all of the following hold. Only one sharp-crested weir or one rectangular opening is active. The coefficients are fixed values. The main free-discharge tick, the four checks and the two records are all complete. If a condition is missing, Module 5 lists what is missing, where to check it and what to enter. These conditions are usually confirmed by the responsible design engineer from drawings or site data."},{"id":"bk-c8-s2","title":"7.2 Limits to enter","chapter":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization","text":"Module 5 constrained-screening settings Figure 7-1 shows the settings of Module 5. Allowable peak outflow Qallow (m³/s) and allowable water level Hallow (m): these should come from regulations or design criteria and be written in the \"Screening-limit basis\". Maximum drain-down time Td and permitted residual percentage: at the event end time Te plus Td, the event-added storage must not exceed this percentage of ΔSmax. Reference level Href: used only to compute the dimensionless parameters ΠQ and ΠS. It is not a safety limit. Search bounds: the minimum and maximum of the weir width b or the opening area Ao. For each ticked size, five equally spaced values are tested within the bounds."},{"id":"bk-c8-s3","title":"7.3 Run and results","chapter":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization","text":"Press \"Run constrained design screening\". The page then switches to the results page. When a feasible case is found, the result lists the selected pond area, the outlet size and a comparison with each limit. Continuous optimization searches the area A, between its minimum and maximum, together with one outlet size, and then refines locally. Its result is not a proven global optimum."},{"id":"bk-c8-s4","title":"7.4 No feasible case","chapter":"Chapter 7  Module 5: constrained design screening and continuous optimization","text":"Blocked diagnosis when no feasible case exists Figure 7-2 shows the screen when no feasible case exists. The system shows \"Blocked\" and the dominant limits (peak outflow, maximum water level or drain-down residual), with \"Go to fix\" buttons. Change the pond area or outlet size on an engineering basis, or widen the search bounds where there is a basis for it. Do not relax Qallow or Hallow just to get a solution."},{"id":"bk-c9","title":"Chapter 8  Reports and traceable records","text":"The \"Run record for this session (read-only)\" on the results page lists the inputs, method, results and limits of this run. \"Download reproducible decision-support report\" saves a text file with the inputs, results and scope of application. You can attach it to the design record. When the inputs change, the old results expire. Run again before downloading. On the \"Start here → Book\" tab you can download this book as a PDF or a single web page, and the input data of the Part II examples.","chapter":"Chapter 8  Reports and traceable records"},{"id":"bk-c10","title":"Chapter 9  Error messages, common questions and the AI assistant","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 9  Error messages, common questions and the AI assistant"},{"id":"bk-c10-s1","title":"9.1 Common questions","chapter":"Chapter 9  Error messages, common questions and the AI assistant","text":"Situation Cause What to do The run button is grey A field in Modules 1 to 3 is unreasonable Read the red list above the button and click a field name to fix it Constrained screening stays locked More than one outlet, coefficients not fixed, or incomplete free-discharge evidence Read the list of missing conditions in Module 5 \"No feasible case\" appears No design within the bounds meets all limits Change the area or outlet according to the dominant limit; see Chapter 18 Mass-balance residual above 0.5 % A numerical problem, often with extreme inputs Check the input units and the datum; see Chapter 13 Results are marked out of date Inputs changed after the run Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again"},{"id":"bk-c10-s2","title":"9.2 AI assistant","chapter":"Chapter 9  Error messages, common questions and the AI assistant","text":"Press \"? Help / AI assistant\" at the bottom left to open the AI assistant. It is an offline help assistant. It runs inside your browser, does not connect to any outside AI service and sends no data. The AI assistant can do three things. Answer operating questions, for example \"What should I do next?\" or \"Why can't I press the button?\". Explain terms, for example \"What is Qallow?\". Search this book: type a keyword (for example \"orifice\", \"RK4\" or \"no feasible\"), and it lists the most relevant sections. Click one to jump to it. The AI assistant does not change your inputs and makes no engineering judgements. It can tell you how the numbers in the results table compare with the limits, but it will never say \"this design can be adopted\"."},{"id":"bk-p12","title":"Part II  Worked Examples","text":"Each chapter of Part II follows the order \"principle → step-by-step example → check → exercises\". The inputs of every example are listed in tables, so you can enter them in the software and get the same numbers as the book. Most of Part II starts from the demonstration case that is loaded when the software opens. Below it is called the \"base case\": Item Value Inflow Triangular, Qp = 0.120 m³/s, Tp = 30 min, Tr = 30 min Pond Constant plan area A = 180 m², floor Hfloor = 0, initial level H0 = 0 Outlet Sharp-crested weir, Cw = 1.70, b = 0.20 m, crest P = 0 Drain-down condition Td = 24 h, permitted residual 1 % Design limits Qallow = 0.080 m³/s, Hallow = 1.00 m When the book says \"start from the base case\", first reset the inputs to the values in the table above. The easiest way is to open \"Start here → Book\", press \"Back to the base case\", and then press \"Run routing and evaluation\" in Module 4. Reloading the web page has the same effect (switch to English again if needed). Both ways clear unsaved changes, so save any case you want to keep as a project first.","chapter":"Part II  Worked Examples"},{"id":"bk-c11","title":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits"},{"id":"bk-c11-s1","title":"10.1 Principle","chapter":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits","text":"After development, the ground surface is less permeable, so the same storm produces a larger and faster flood peak. A detention pond stores part of the water and releases it slowly, so the peak seen downstream becomes smaller and later. A design usually has two limits. Allowable outflow Qallow: the largest outflow allowed by the downstream drainage system or by regulations. Allowable water level Hallow: the upper limit of the water level in the pond, usually set by the pond depth, the freeboard or the surrounding ground levels. A design is \"good enough\" only when Qp,o ≤ Qallow and Hmax ≤ Hallow at the same time, and the event-added storage left in the pond after the required drain-down time Td does not exceed the declared percentage."},{"id":"bk-c11-s2","title":"10.2 Example: is the base case good enough?","chapter":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits","text":"This example assumes Qallow = 0.080 m³/s and Hallow = 1.00 m (teaching assumption). Open PondGate and keep the base case (if you have changed any input, press \"Back to the base case\" first). In Module 5, enter Qallow = 0.080 and Hallow = 1.00. Go back to Module 4 and press \"Run routing and evaluation\". Inflow and outflow hydrographs of the base case Figure 10-1 shows the inflow and outflow hydrographs of the base case. The results page reads as follows. Item Result Limit Reading Peak outflow Qp,o 0.0903 m³/s Qallow = 0.0800 m³/s Exceeds Peak water level Hmax 0.413 m Hallow = 1.000 m Within Peak ratio RQ 0.753 — About 25 % peak reduction Drain-down residual (Te + Td) 0.03 % 1.00 % Within Event-added detention volume ΔSmax 74.385 m³ — — The \"Beginner check\" box says \"Not good enough\", because the peak outflow exceeds the allowable value. The water level still has a large margin. This means the pond is not too small; the outlet is too large and releases water too fast. Chapter 14 uses the same case to find a direction for improvement."},{"id":"bk-c11-s3","title":"10.3 Key points","chapter":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits","text":"To judge \"good enough\", read the outflow, the water level and the drain-down together. Reading only one of them can mislead you. When one limit is exceeded and another has a large margin, the pond area and the outlet are usually not well balanced."},{"id":"bk-c11-s4","title":"10.4 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 10  The role of a detention pond and the design limits","text":"Change Qallow to 0.100 m³/s. Is the base case good enough? Why does Qp,o exceed the limit although Hmax is only 0.413 m? Explain in one sentence."},{"id":"bk-c12","title":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage"},{"id":"bk-c12-s1","title":"11.1 Principle","chapter":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","text":"The change of the water volume in the pond equals the inflow minus the outflow: dS/dt = Qin(t) − Qout(H) With a constant plan area, the storage is proportional to the water level: S(H) = A × (H − Hfloor) Together these two equations are called \"level-pool routing\". It assumes that the water surface is level at every moment. It suits detention ponds with a large area, shallow water and low velocity."},{"id":"bk-c12-s2","title":"11.2 Hand calculation: inflow volume","chapter":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","text":"The volume of a triangular hydrograph is the area of the triangle: Vin = ½ × Qp × (Tp + Tr) = ½ × 0.120 × 3600 = 216 m³ The software computes an event-added detention volume ΔSmax = 74.385 m³, so the storage ratio is RS = 74.385 ÷ 216 = 0.344, the same as in the results table. In other words, at most about one third of the inflow volume was in the pond at the same time."},{"id":"bk-c12-s3","title":"11.3 An important property: the outflow peak lies on the falling limb of the inflow","chapter":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","text":"When the outflow depends only on the water level in the pond (free discharge), dS/dt = 0 at the moment of the highest level, so Qin = Qout. Therefore the outflow peak always lies on the falling limb of the inflow hydrograph. Check with the base case: an independent program puts the outflow peak at t = 37.4 min, with Qout = 0.0903 m³/s. The inflow at that moment is: Qin = 0.120 × (60 − 37.4) ÷ 30 = 0.0904 m³/s The two differ by less than 0.0001 m³/s; the difference comes from rounding the time to 0.1 min. In Figure 10-1 the highest point of the outflow curve touches the falling line of the inflow. This is the property at work."},{"id":"bk-c12-s4","title":"11.4 Key points","chapter":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","text":"The moment of the highest water level is the moment of the largest outflow, and also the moment when Qin = Qout. If another program shows an outflow peak that is not on the falling limb of the inflow, first check whether the outlet is affected by downstream conditions."},{"id":"bk-c12-s5","title":"11.5 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 11  Hydraulic basics: the continuity equation and storage","text":"If Tp = Tr = 45 min and Qp is unchanged, what is the inflow volume? With A = 180 m², how much water-level rise does ΔSmax = 74.385 m³ represent? Compare it with Hmax."},{"id":"bk-c13","title":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings"},{"id":"bk-c13-s1","title":"12.1 Sharp-crested weir","chapter":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings","text":"Qout = Cw × b × (H − P)^1.5 In the base case, Cw × b = 1.70 × 0.20 = 0.34. Table 12-1 lists the discharge at different heads. Head H − P (m) 0.10 0.20 0.30 0.40 0.50 Weir flow Qout (m³/s) 0.0108 0.0304 0.0559 0.0860 0.1202 The discharge grows with the 1.5 power of the head, so a small rise in level greatly increases the outflow."},{"id":"bk-c13-s2","title":"12.2 Rectangular opening","chapter":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings","text":"PondGate computes a rectangular opening in two ranges. The opening height is a, the width is w = Ao ÷ a, and the head is y = H − invert elevation. The water has not risen above the top of the opening (y ≤ a): the opening acts as a weir of width w, Q = Cw × w × y^1.5. The water is above the top of the opening (y > a): the discharge starts from the weir flow at the top of the opening and adds the increase given by the orifice equation. The discharge is therefore continuous at the transition point and does not jump: Q = Cw × w × a^1.5 + Cd × w × a × [ √(2g(y − a/2)) − √(2g × a/2) ] Take Ao = 0.010 m², a = 0.10 m, invert 0.05 m, Cw = 1.70 and Cd = 0.62 as an example. Table 12-2 compares the discharges of the weir and the opening. Water level H (m) 0.15 0.30 0.50 0.75 1.00 Weir b = 0.20 m (m³/s) 0.0198 0.0559 0.1202 0.2208 0.3400 Opening Ao = 0.010 m² (m³/s) 0.0054 0.0115 0.0166 0.0214 0.0253 The discharge of the opening grows only with about the 0.5 power of the head. As the level rises, a weir releases water quickly, while an opening releases it slowly. This difference drives the design behaviour in Chapters 14 and 15."},{"id":"bk-c13-s3","title":"12.3 Coefficients and free discharge","chapter":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings","text":"Cw = 1.70 and Cd = 0.62 are common preliminary values. The coefficients of a real structure depend on its shape, edges and approach velocity. A formal design should use calibrated values. All these equations assume free discharge: the downstream water level is low enough not to back up into the outlet. If the outlet may be submerged from downstream, the equations no longer hold. This is why constrained screening requires the free-discharge evidence."},{"id":"bk-c13-s4","title":"12.4 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 12  Outlet equations: weirs and rectangular openings","text":"If the weir width is doubled, by what factor does the weir flow change at the same head? When the water level at the opening rises from 0.50 m to 1.00 m, by what percentage does the discharge increase? And for the weir?"},{"id":"bk-c14","title":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance"},{"id":"bk-c14-s1","title":"13.1 Principle","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"The continuity equation has no general analytical solution, so it is integrated numerically step by step. PondGate uses the fourth-order Runge–Kutta method (RK4), which computes four slopes in each step: k1 = f(t, S) k2 = f(t + Δt/2, S + Δt × k1/2) k3 = f(t + Δt/2, S + Δt × k2/2) k4 = f(t + Δt, S + Δt × k3) S(next) = S + Δt/6 × (k1 + 2k2 + 2k3 + k4) Here f(t, S) = Qin(t) − Qout(H(S)), and the PondGate step is Δt = 2 s."},{"id":"bk-c14-s2","title":"13.2 One step by hand","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"Take the base case at t = 1800 s (the time of the inflow peak). At this moment S = 65.608 m³, H = 0.3645 m, Qin = 0.1200 m³/s and Qout = 0.0748 m³/s. Slope Value (m³/s) k1 0.045181 k2 0.045037 k3 0.045038 k4 0.044894 Substituting gives S_next = 65.608 + 2 ÷ 6 × (0.045181 + 2 × 0.045037 + 2 × 0.045038 + 0.044894) = 65.699 m³, and the water level rises to 0.3650 m. The four slopes are very close, which shows that a 2-second step is very small for this case."},{"id":"bk-c14-s3","title":"13.3 Time-step sensitivity","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"Table 13-1 compares results for different steps from an independent program. RK4 hardly changes for steps up to 60 s. The simple forward Euler method loses accuracy quickly as the step grows. Method and step Qp,o (m³/s) Hmax (m) RK4, 2 s (PondGate) 0.09032 0.4132 RK4, 60 s 0.09028 0.4131 RK4, 300 s 0.08896 0.4091 Euler, 60 s 0.09179 0.4177 Euler, 300 s 0.09910 0.4396 The peaks for large steps in the table are also affected by sampling: with a 300-second step, the result is seen only every 5 minutes."},{"id":"bk-c14-s4","title":"13.4 Mass-balance residual","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"The mass-balance residual ε/Vin checks whether \"cumulative inflow − cumulative outflow\" equals the change in storage. For the base case the absolute residual is 1.259 × 10⁻¹⁰ m³, and the relative residual is shown as 0.000 %. Above 0.5 %, the results must not be used; check the inputs first."},{"id":"bk-c14-s5","title":"13.5 How the numbers in this book are checked","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"Every example in Part II was run in the software by docs/book/run_examples.js, which read the results from the screen. docs/book/check_examples.py then recomputed them with an independently written RK4 program. The two agree on Qp,o, Hmax, ΔSmax and the drain-down residual in every case (Qp,o differs by less than 0.00005 m³/s and Hmax by less than 0.0005 m). When one number is changed on purpose, the check program reports an error."},{"id":"bk-c14-s6","title":"13.6 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 13  Numerical method: RK4, time step and mass balance","text":"Why are the outflow peaks for large steps too low? Hint: think about the sampling times. If the mass-balance residual shows 2 %, which inputs should you check first?"},{"id":"bk-c15","title":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results"},{"id":"bk-c15-s1","title":"14.1 Problem","chapter":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","text":"In the base case of Chapter 10 the outflow is too large (0.0903 m³/s > 0.080 m³/s). There are two ways to adjust it: change the weir width b, or change the pond area A. This chapter changes one variable at a time and watches how the results change."},{"id":"bk-c15-s2","title":"14.2 Steps","chapter":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","text":"Start from the base case (Start here → Book → press \"Back to the base case\", or reload the page). In Module 3, change the weir width b to 0.10 m, press \"Run routing and evaluation\" and record the results. Change b to 0.40 m, press \"Run routing and evaluation\" and record the results. Press \"Back to the base case\" (b returns to 0.20 m). Then change the area A in Module 2 to 120 m² and press \"Run routing and evaluation\". Change A to 360 m² and press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again."},{"id":"bk-c15-s3","title":"14.3 Results","chapter":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","text":"Change Qp,o (m³/s) Hmax (m) ΔSmax (m³) RQ b = 0.10 m 0.0732 0.570 102.635 0.610 Base case b = 0.20 m, A = 180 m² 0.0903 0.413 74.385 0.753 b = 0.40 m 0.1027 0.284 51.045 0.856 A = 120 m² 0.1015 0.447 53.590 0.846 A = 360 m² 0.0636 0.327 117.754 0.530 Hydrographs with a weir width of 0.10 m Figure 14-1 shows the hydrographs for b = 0.10 m. Compared with Figure 10-1, the outflow is flatter and later."},{"id":"bk-c15-s4","title":"14.4 Interpretation","chapter":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","text":"A narrower weir: the outflow falls, but the water level rises. The water is \"held\" in the pond, so more detention volume is needed. A larger area: both the outflow and the water level fall. The price is more land. Both b = 0.10 m and A = 360 m² bring Qp,o below 0.080 m³/s. With the first, the level rises to 0.570 m; with the second, only to 0.327 m. Which is better depends on the land cost and the allowable level. This is the direction that the \"Beginner check\" box suggests: a larger pond area usually lowers both the peak water level and the peak outflow; a smaller outlet lowers the peak outflow but raises the peak water level."},{"id":"bk-c15-s5","title":"14.5 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 14  Example: how the weir width and pond area affect the results","text":"Using the base case, find the largest weir width that brings Qp,o just below 0.080 m³/s (to 0.01 m). If Hallow = 0.50 m, which designs in the table above also meet Qallow = 0.080 m³/s?"},{"id":"bk-c16","title":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time"},{"id":"bk-c16-s1","title":"15.1 Problem","chapter":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","text":"Replace the outlet of the base case with a rectangular opening (opening height 0.10 m, invert 0.05 m, Cd = 0.62) and compare three opening areas."},{"id":"bk-c16-s2","title":"15.2 Steps","chapter":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","text":"Start from the base case (press \"Back to the base case\"). In Module 3, untick \"Sharp-crested weir\" and tick \"Rectangular opening\". Enter the opening area Ao as 0.005, 0.010 and 0.020 m² in turn, and press \"Run routing and evaluation\" after each one."},{"id":"bk-c16-s3","title":"15.3 Results","chapter":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","text":"Ao (m²) Qp,o (m³/s) Hmax (m) ΔSmax (m³) Storage left at Te + Td 0.005 0.0130 1.056 190.013 5.01 % 0.010 0.0243 0.936 168.476 5.41 % 0.020 0.0428 0.752 135.278 6.67 % Hydrographs with an opening of 0.010 m² Figure 15-1 shows the hydrographs for Ao = 0.010 m². The outflow is very flat and continues long after the event ends."},{"id":"bk-c16-s4","title":"15.4 Interpretation","chapter":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","text":"All three openings keep the outflow peak far below 0.080 m³/s, so the peak reduction is strong. But the water levels are all high. With Ao = 0.005 m² the level even exceeds the allowable 1.00 m. The drain-down matters even more: after 24 hours, more than 5 % of the event-added storage is still in the pond, above the declared 1 %. The main reason is the opening invert of 0.05 m. The water below the opening, 0.05 m × 180 m² = 9 m³, can never flow out, and this is about 5 % of ΔSmax. In addition, the opening releases water very slowly at low levels (Chapter 12). The pond does not drain, so less volume is available when the next storm comes. This chapter shows that looking only at the outflow peak can lead to a design that does not drain. This is why PondGate treats the drain-down residual as a screening limit. The \"Beginner check\" also lists the drain-down residual as its third item, and all three openings show \"exceeds limit\" there."},{"id":"bk-c16-s5","title":"15.5 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 15  Example: rectangular openings and drain-down time","text":"When the opening area is doubled, by about what factor does Qp,o change? Why is it not exactly 2? If the drain-down time Td is changed to 48 h, does the residual for Ao = 0.010 m² become larger or smaller? Check it in the software."},{"id":"bk-c17","title":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration"},{"id":"bk-c17-s1","title":"16.1 Problem","chapter":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","text":"Three inflows all have a peak of 0.120 m³/s. The pond and weir are those of the base case; only the inflow shape changes. Inflow Settings Inflow volume Short triangle (base case) Tp = Tr = 30 min 216 m³ Long flat-topped Rise 24, plateau 72, recession 24 min 691.2 m³ Long triangle Tp = Tr = 96 min 691.2 m³"},{"id":"bk-c17-s2","title":"16.2 Steps","chapter":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","text":"Long flat-topped: in Module 1, set \"Standard case\" to the long-duration event, keep the other defaults and press \"Run routing and evaluation\". Long triangle: set the triangular event again, change both Tp and Tr to 96 min and press \"Run routing and evaluation\"."},{"id":"bk-c17-s3","title":"16.3 Results","chapter":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","text":"Inflow Qp,o (m³/s) RQ Hmax (m) ΔSmax (m³) FPI95 Short triangle 0.0903 0.753 0.413 74.385 0.006 Long flat-topped 0.1200 1.000 0.499 89.890 0.036 Long triangle 0.1123 0.936 0.478 86.018 0.011 Hydrographs of the long-duration event Figure 16-1 shows the hydrographs of the long flat-topped event. The plateau lasts 72 minutes. The pond fills during the plateau, and the outflow catches up with the inflow."},{"id":"bk-c17-s4","title":"16.4 Interpretation","chapter":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","text":"The peaks are the same, but the results differ a lot. The long flat-topped event has RQ = 1.000, which means this pond gives no peak reduction at all. The reason is the limited capacity of the pond. When the water keeps coming long enough, the pond fills to the equilibrium level where \"outflow equals inflow\", and the detention effect disappears. The long-duration events have a larger FPI95, which means the top of the outflow is flat. The \"time of peak\" then has little meaning; use the median-volume time and the discharge-centroid time instead. So a design cannot rely on the peak discharge alone. For the same peak, check the most unfavourable duration and shape."},{"id":"bk-c17-s5","title":"16.5 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 16  Example: inflow shape and duration","text":"In the long flat-topped event, what is the water level when the outflow equals the inflow? Work it back from the weir equation in Chapter 12. If the plateau time is changed to 36 minutes, how does RQ change?"},{"id":"bk-c18","title":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table"},{"id":"bk-c18-s1","title":"17.1 Problem","chapter":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","text":"Real ponds often have sloped sides or irregular shapes, so the area changes with the level. In that case, describe the pond with a stage–storage table. This example uses the data of Table 17-1; all other conditions are the base case. Water level H (m) 0 0.25 0.50 0.75 1.00 1.50 Storage S (m³) 0 30 80 150 240 420 This table is also in the download package as ch17_irregular_stage_storage.csv."},{"id":"bk-c18-s2","title":"17.2 Steps","chapter":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","text":"Start from the base case (press \"Back to the base case\"). In Module 2, set the storage representation to the tabulated stage–storage relation. Paste Table 17-1 into the table field (one \"level, storage\" pair per line), or import the CSV file. Press \"Run routing and evaluation\"."},{"id":"bk-c18-s3","title":"17.3 Results","chapter":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","text":"Pond shape Qp,o (m³/s) Hmax (m) ΔSmax (m³) Constant area 180 m² (base case) 0.0903 0.413 74.385 Irregular pond (Table 17-1) 0.0912 0.416 63.161"},{"id":"bk-c18-s4","title":"17.4 Interpretation","chapter":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","text":"The irregular pond is smaller at low levels (the mean area between 0 and 0.25 m is only 120 m²). The water level rises faster, so the outlet starts to release a lot of water earlier. At 0.416 m the irregular pond holds only about 63 m³, while the constant-area pond holds about 75 m³. Less water stays in the pond and more is released, so Qp,o is slightly higher and the detention volume is about 11 m³ smaller, while the peak levels are almost the same. When you use a stage–storage table, note the following: The first row is usually the pond floor (S = 0). Routing does not extrapolate beyond the table. The highest level in the table must be above the highest possible water level; otherwise the result is invalid. The table and the outlet elevations must use the same datum."},{"id":"bk-c18-s5","title":"17.5 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 17  Example: an irregular pond and a stage–storage table","text":"From Table 17-1, compute the mean area between 0.25 and 0.50 m. What happens if the table only reaches H = 0.40 m?"},{"id":"bk-c19","title":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\""},{"id":"bk-c19-s1","title":"18.1 Problem","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"In Chapter 14 you found a few feasible designs by manual trial and error. This chapter uses the constrained design screening of Module 5 instead, so the software searches the declared range automatically. The limits are the same as in Chapter 10: Qallow = 0.080 m³/s, Hallow = 1.00 m and a residual of no more than 1 % within 24 hours."},{"id":"bk-c19-s2","title":"18.2 Preparation: free-discharge evidence","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"The free-discharge evidence must be complete before constrained screening. This example is a teaching case, so the ticks and records are teaching assumptions. Start from the base case (Start here → Book → press \"Back to the base case\"). In Module 3, tick \"Free-discharge boundary confirmed\" and the four checks. In the \"Free-discharge evidence record\", enter: Teaching assumption (practice only): a single outlet with free discharge, not affected by tailwater, backwater, submergence or operational control. In the \"Screening-limit basis\", enter: Teaching assumption (practice only): Qallow and Hallow are example values set in this book. In a real project these two records must give the drawing or inspection number, the checker and the date, and the regulatory source of the limit values."},{"id":"bk-c19-s3","title":"18.3 Example 1: minimum detention volume","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"In Module 5, enter Qallow = 0.080 and Hallow = 1.00, set the objective to \"Minimum event-added detention volume ΔSmax\", and keep the weir-width bounds at 0.05 to 1.00 m. Press \"Run constrained design screening\". Screening result (minimum detention volume) Figure 18-1 shows the screening result. The software searches areas from 60 to 600 m² (on a 20 m² grid) and the declared weir-width range, and then runs 2 passes of local refinement. Item Result Plan area A 460 m² Weir width b 0.53 m (the input field shows 0.525) Event-added detention volume ΔSmax 92.0 m³ Peak outflow / allowable 0.0798 / 0.0800 m³/s Peak water level / allowable 0.200 / 1.000 m Drain-down residual 0.07 / 1.00 %"},{"id":"bk-c19-s4","title":"18.4 Example 2: minimum area","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"Change the objective to \"Minimum plan area A\" and press \"Run constrained design screening\" again. Item Result Plan area A 100 m² Weir width b 0.05 m (at the declared lower bound) Event-added detention volume ΔSmax 94.6 m³ Peak outflow / allowable 0.0782 / 0.0800 m³/s Peak water level / allowable 0.946 / 1.000 m"},{"id":"bk-c19-s5","title":"18.5 Reading the two results","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"Both designs meet all limits, but the trade-offs are completely different. The minimum-volume design has very shallow water (0.200 m) and needs a large area. The minimum-area design needs only 100 m², but the water depth is close to the limit (0.946 m). In the minimum-area design the weir width stops at the lower bound of 0.05 m, and the screen marks it as a boundary case. This means the pond might be smaller still if a narrower weir were allowed. But a very narrow weir blocks easily, so the lower bound should follow the maintenance needs, not be relaxed to get a better number. Both results are only conditional screening within the declared range. Neither is a global optimum or a final design."},{"id":"bk-c19-s6","title":"18.6 Example 3: Blocked","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"Make the limits very strict: Qallow = 0.040 m³/s and Hallow = 0.30 m. Set the objective back to minimum detention volume and press \"Run constrained design screening\" again. The result says: \"No feasible screening case was found within the declared physical bounds and discrete screening domain. Dominant constraints observed: peak-outflow limit, maximum-water-level limit, drain-down residual limit.\" This block is reasonable. To bring a peak of 0.120 m³/s down to 0.040 m³/s, the pond must store a lot of water. But the level must not exceed 0.30 m, and even the upper area bound of 600 m² is not enough. The right way to respond: First check that the sources of Qallow and Hallow are correct. If they are, do not relax them just to get a solution. Based on the dominant limits, consider adjustments with an engineering basis: for example, a larger area range if the land allows it, another outlet type, or several smaller facilities. If a larger area is needed, use continuous optimization with a larger area range and check again."},{"id":"bk-c19-s7","title":"18.7 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 18  The full design workflow: from failing to feasible, and reading \"Blocked\"","text":"In Example 1, Qp,o = 0.0798 m³/s, very close to 0.080. Why do screening results often lie close to a limit? In Example 3, relax only Hallow to 1.00 m (Qallow stays at 0.040) and run it in the software. Is it still blocked?"},{"id":"bk-c20","title":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM"},{"id":"bk-c20-s1","title":"19.1 Why cross-check","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"Different programs use different numerical methods. If the inputs are exactly the same but the results differ a lot, at least one side has an error in its inputs, units or settings. A cross-check can find such errors, but it only shows \"numerical consistency\". It does not show that the results match field conditions."},{"id":"bk-c20-s2","title":"19.2 Built-in example: E-W1 A-005","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"PondGate has a read-only verification case, E-W1 A-005. It is a laboratory-scale case with a single weir. The event lasts from 0 to 977.25 s, and the initial level is H0 = 0.07399 m. The stage–storage and outflow equations of the case are fixed on the page and do not change your normal routing inputs. On the \"Results, verification and records\" page, find the \"E-W1 A-005 external cross-check benchmark\". Press \"Run E-W1 A-005 benchmark RK4\". Then press \"Time-step sensitivity (0.125 / 0.250 / 0.500 s)\"."},{"id":"bk-c20-s3","title":"19.3 Comparison of three engines","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"The three-engine table on the page compares the stored results of PondGate (DSS RK4, Δt = 0.25 s), EPA SWMM 5.2.4 and HEC-HMS 4.13 over a common window of 3,910 time points. Engine Peak outflow (m³/s) Peak time (s) PondGate RK4 0.00613899 154.50 HEC-HMS 4.13 0.00613899 154.50 EPA SWMM 5.2.4 0.00614476 154.75 Pair Outflow RMSE (m³/s) HEC-HMS and PondGate 8.57 × 10⁻⁹ HEC-HMS and SWMM 2.30 × 10⁻⁶ PondGate and SWMM 2.30 × 10⁻⁶ The peaks of the three engines differ by less than 0.1 %. HEC-HMS uses the Modified Puls method. To match its one-minute computation interval, the case uses a volume-conserving conversion of time and discharge: one HEC minute stands for 0.25 s of the original record, the HEC discharge is 1/240 of the original discharge, and the output is converted back to the original scale."},{"id":"bk-c20-s4","title":"19.4 Time-step sensitivity","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"RK4 step Maximum outflow (m³/s) Mass residual (m³) 0.125 s 6.13899 × 10⁻³ 2.49 × 10⁻¹⁰ 0.250 s 6.13899 × 10⁻³ 9.98 × 10⁻¹⁰ 0.500 s 6.13893 × 10⁻³ −7.93 × 10⁻⁵ When the step grows from 0.125 s to 0.500 s, the maximum outflow changes by only about 0.001 %, so a 0.25-second step is enough."},{"id":"bk-c20-s5","title":"19.5 Compare your own case","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"Build the same pond in HEC-HMS or SWMM: the same inflow, initial level, stage–storage relation, outlet equation and coefficients. Save the actual simulation results of the external model as a CSV. The first row holds the column names. The required columns are time_s (or time, t) and outflow_m3_s (or qout, qo); a full comparison also needs incremental_storage_m3. Press \"Download external-output CSV template\" for an example of the format. In the \"External-model cross check\", choose the reference hydrograph (the current routing result or one of the public benchmarks A-005 to D-005) and the external model, import the CSV and press \"Compare external result\". Common causes of disagreement are different units (L/s and m³/s), different time bases (minutes and seconds), different datums and different coefficients in the external model."},{"id":"bk-c20-s6","title":"19.6 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 19  Cross-checks with HEC-HMS and SWMM","text":"Why is the difference between HEC-HMS and PondGate much smaller than that between SWMM and PondGate? Hint: look at the data and methods each one uses. If the outflow peak of the external model is 240 times larger than that of PondGate, what is the most likely error?"},{"id":"bk-c21","title":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"This chapter brings the earlier chapters together in one complete check. The site and the numbers are a fictitious teaching case.","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site"},{"id":"bk-c21-s1","title":"20.1 Case conditions","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"The post-development design inflow hydrograph of a development site is given in Table 20-1 (ch20_site_inflow.csv in the download package). Time (min) 0 10 20 30 40 50 60 80 100 120 Discharge (m³/s) 0 0.03 0.09 0.21 0.25 0.18 0.12 0.06 0.025 0 Design limits (teaching assumption): Qallow = 0.15 m³/s, Hallow = 0.80 m, and a residual of no more than 1 % within 24 hours. The outlet is a sharp-crested weir with Cw = 1.70 and the crest at the pond floor."},{"id":"bk-c21-s2","title":"20.2 Step 1: compute the inflow volume by hand","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"Use the trapezoidal rule: multiply the mean discharge of each pair of neighbouring points by the time interval, and add them up: Vin = Σ (Qi + Qi+1) ÷ 2 × Δti = 666 m³ You can use this number to check whether the RS values later are reasonable."},{"id":"bk-c21-s3","title":"20.3 Step 2: enter the hydrograph and try a design","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"In Module 1, set \"Standard case\" to the user-defined event and the inflow shape to imported time-discharge pairs. Paste Table 20-1 (one \"time, discharge\" pair per line). In Module 2, enter A = 300 m²; in Module 3, enter the weir width b = 0.30 m. In Module 5, enter Qallow = 0.15 and Hallow = 0.80. Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" and read the \"Beginner check\". Trial A (m²) b (m) Qp,o (m³/s) Hmax (m) ΔSmax (m³) Reading 1 300 0.30 0.2037 0.542 162.703 Outflow exceeds 2 500 0.30 0.1662 0.474 236.763 Outflow still exceeds For trial 1, RS = 162.703 ÷ 666 = 0.244, the same as in the results table. A larger area lowers the outflow, but not enough, while the water level still has a large margin. From the lesson of Chapter 14, the weir should be made narrower at the same time."},{"id":"bk-c21-s4","title":"20.4 Step 3: constrained screening","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"Complete the free-discharge evidence (teaching assumption, as in Chapter 18). In Module 5, set the weir-width bounds to 0.10 to 0.60 m and the objective to \"Minimum event-added detention volume ΔSmax\". Press \"Run constrained design screening\". Screening result of the capstone project Figure 20-1 shows the screening result. Item Result Plan area A 520 m² Weir width b 0.23 m (the input field shows 0.225) Event-added detention volume ΔSmax 274.5 m³ Peak outflow / allowable 0.1467 / 0.1500 m³/s Peak water level / allowable 0.528 / 0.800 m Drain-down residual 0.17 / 1.00 %"},{"id":"bk-c21-s5","title":"20.5 Step 4: check and record","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"Check that the mass-balance residual is close to 0 %. Use the property from Chapter 11: the outflow peak should lie on the falling limb of the inflow. Press \"Download reproducible decision-support report\" and file the report together with Table 20-1 and the basis of the limits. Readers who are signed in can save this case as a project and share it with the colleague who reviews it."},{"id":"bk-c21-s6","title":"20.6 Step 5: write the conclusion","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"A good conclusion gives the source of the inputs, the source of the limits, the selected sizes, the comparison with the limits and the scope of application. For example: \"For the design inflow of Table 20-1, Qallow = 0.15 m³/s and Hallow = 0.80 m, a detention pond with an area of 520 m² and a weir width of 0.23 m gives a peak outflow of 0.1467 m³/s, a peak water level of 0.528 m and a drain-down residual of 0.17 %, all within the limits. This result assumes free discharge and fixed coefficients. The freeboard, structural safety and blockage risk must be checked separately.\""},{"id":"bk-c21-s7","title":"20.7 Exercises","chapter":"Chapter 20  Capstone project: checking the detention pond of a site","text":"If the site allows a pond of only 400 m², screen again with the minimum-area objective and see whether a feasible case exists. Write a short conclusion that explains why the pond size cannot be decided from the peak inflow of 0.25 m³/s alone."},{"id":"bk-c22","title":"Appendix A  Terms and symbols","text":"Symbol Term Chinese term Unit Qp, Qp,i Peak inflow 入流尖峰 m³/s Qp,o Peak outflow 出流尖峰 m³/s Tp, Tr Time to peak, recession time 洪峰到達時間、退水時間 min T, Te Event duration, event end 總事件延時、事件結束時間 min Vin Inflow volume 入流體積 m³ S(H) Stage–storage relation 水位—蓄水量關係 m³ A Plan area 平面面積 m² Hfloor Floor elevation 池底高程 m H0 Initial level 初始水位 m Hmax Peak water level 最高水位 m ΔSmax Event-added detention volume 事件新增滯洪量 m³ Cw Weir coefficient 堰流係數 — b Weir width 堰寬 m P Crest elevation 堰頂高程 m Cd Discharge coefficient 流量係數 — Ao Opening area 開口面積 m² RQ Peak ratio Qp,o ÷ Qp,i 尖峰比 — RS Storage ratio ΔSmax ÷ Vin 蓄水比 — RT Normalized lag 無因次遲滯 — FPI95 Near-peak flatness index 近洪峰平坦指標 — ε/Vin Mass-balance residual 質量平衡殘差 % Qallow Allowable peak outflow 容許出流尖峰 m³/s Hallow Allowable water level 容許水位 m Td Maximum drain-down time 最長排空時間 h Href Reference level 參考水位 m Δt Time step 時間步長 s For fuller explanations of the terms, see \"Start here → Glossary\" in the software.","chapter":"Appendix A  Terms and symbols"},{"id":"bk-c23","title":"Appendix B  Error messages and what to do","text":"Message or situation on screen Meaning What to do The run button is grey, with a red list above it A field is unreasonable Click a field name in the list and fix the field outlined in red \"Screening not yet run\" Only normal routing was done For normal routing read the \"Beginner check\"; to screen, go to Module 5 Module 5 lists missing conditions The conditions for constrained screening are not met Complete what the list says to check and to enter \"No feasible screening case was found …\" No feasible design within the bounds Adjust according to the dominant limits; see Chapter 18 Results are marked out of date Inputs changed after the run Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" again Mass-balance residual above 0.5 % A numerical problem Check the units, the datum and the range of the stage–storage table Importing time–discharge pairs fails Format error The first time is 0, times increase, discharges are not negative, at least two lines No results after opening a project A project stores only the inputs Press \"Run routing and evaluation\" in Module 4 A viewer cannot save Role setting Ask the company administrator to change the role, or ask an engineer to save","chapter":"Appendix B  Error messages and what to do"},{"id":"bk-c24","title":"Appendix C  Answers to the exercises","text":"All numerical answers were obtained by running this version of the software and were checked with an independent program. For explanation questions, the key points are given. Exercise Answer 10.1 Good enough (preliminary reading). Qp,o = 0.0903 ≤ 0.100 m³/s, Hmax = 0.413 ≤ 1.00 m and the drain-down residual 0.03 % ≤ 1 %. The beginner check says \"Preliminary reading: all three limits are met\". 10.2 A 0.20 m weir releases water too fast. The water flows away before it is stored, so the capacity of the pond is not fully used. 11.1 Vin = ½ × 0.120 × 5400 = 324 m³. 11.2 74.385 ÷ 180 = 0.413 m, equal to Hmax, because the floor and the initial level are both 0. 12.1 2 times. Weir flow is proportional to the weir width. 12.2 The opening rises from 0.0166 to 0.0253 m³/s, about 52 %. The weir rises from 0.1202 to 0.3400 m³/s, about 183 %. 13.1 A large step samples only at the end of each step, so it can miss the true peak. A larger step also gives a larger numerical error. 13.2 The units (L/s and m³/s, minutes and seconds), the datum, whether the stage–storage table covers the highest level, and any extreme or unreasonable input. 14.1 With b = 0.12 m, Qp,o = 0.0780 m³/s (Hmax = 0.527 m). With b = 0.13 m, Qp,o = 0.0801 m³/s, which exceeds the limit. So the largest weir width is 0.12 m. 14.2 Only A = 360 m² (Qp,o = 0.0636 m³/s, Hmax = 0.327 m). For b = 0.10 m, Hmax = 0.570 m, which exceeds 0.50 m. 15.1 It changes from 0.0243 to 0.0428 m³/s, about 1.76 times. With a larger opening the water level in the pond is lower, so the head is smaller, and the discharge of an opening grows only with about the 0.5 power of the head. 15.2 It becomes only slightly smaller: from 5.41 % to 5.36 %. The 9 m³ below the opening invert can never drain, so a longer drain-down time still cannot meet the 1 % limit. 16.1 0.120 = 0.34 × H^1.5, so H = (0.120 ÷ 0.34)^(2/3) = 0.499 m, exactly the Hmax of the software. 16.2 Qp,o = 0.1191 m³/s, RQ = 0.993, Hmax = 0.497 m. Even with half the plateau, the peak reduction is still less than 1 %, because 36 minutes is still long enough for the pond to approach the equilibrium level. 17.1 (80 − 30) ÷ 0.25 = 200 m². 17.2 If the peak level goes beyond the table, routing does not extrapolate, the S(H) boundary check shows a block and the result must not be used. Add data for higher levels. 18.1 The objective is the minimum detention volume. A larger outflow means less water must be stored, so the best point pushes the outflow up to just below Qallow. 18.2 It is not blocked. Screening finds A = 260 m² and b = 0.05 m (at the lower bound), with Qp,o = 0.0393 m³/s, Hmax = 0.598 m and a drain-down residual of 0.70 %. The block in Example 3 came mainly from Hallow = 0.30 m; but limits may only be changed on a regulatory or design basis. 19.1 HEC-HMS uses the same stage–storage and outflow tables as PondGate, so the difference comes only from the numerical method. SWMM uses its own storage curve and outlet representation, so the difference is larger, but the peaks still differ by less than 0.1 %. 19.2 Most likely the time and discharge scale conversion (laboratory scale factor 240) was not applied. Check the comparison-scale setting and the units of the external model. 20.1 Yes. The minimum-area objective selects A = 380 m² and b = 0.12 m (the input field shows 0.123), with Qp,o = 0.1392 m³/s, Hmax = 0.761 m and a drain-down residual of 0.20 %. With a smaller area, the water level is closer to the 0.80 m limit. 20.2 Key point: for the same peak, a different inflow duration and shape give a different inflow volume (Chapter 16). In this case the inflow volume is 666 m³, and the storage needed depends on both the volume and the release capacity of the outlet.","chapter":"Appendix C  Answers to the exercises"},{"id":"bk-c25","title":"Appendix D  References and software citation","text":"PondGate source code: GitHub hongyaoming-cloud/detention-pond-dss (MIT licence). PondGate v1.0.0: DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23296722. PondGate v1.0.1: DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23298538. PondGate v1.0.2: DOI 10.5281/zenodo.23299011. PondGate, all versions (concept DOI): 10.5281/zenodo.23296721, which resolves to the latest version. U.S. Army Corps of Engineers, HEC-HMS 4.13 user's manual (Modified Puls routing). U.S. EPA, Storm Water Management Model (SWMM) 5.2 user's manual. Suggested citation: Hong, Y.-M. PondGate: open-source detention-pond routing software (version 1.3.4). Zenodo. Use the version DOI shown on the Zenodo page. Related journal papers will be added to a later edition after publication.","chapter":"Appendix D  References and software citation"}],"downloads":{"pdf":"book/PondGate_book_v1.3.4_en.pdf","zip":"book/PondGate_book_v1.3.4_examples_en.zip"}};
